Table loyalty_coupon {
  lcpId String [pk]
  lcpCompId String [not null]
  lcpBranchId String [note: 'Branch that ISSUED it.']
  lcpTenantId String
  lcpAccYear String [not null, note: 'FY of issue. Reporting filter only.']
  lcpLcbId String [not null]
  lcpCode String [not null, note: 'What the counter scans or types.']
  lcpPin String [note: 'So a code photographed off someone\'s phone screen is not by itself
spendable.']
  lcpCustId String [note: 'NULL = bearer instrument. Non-null and a non-transferable batch means
this customer alone may spend it.']
  lcpMemberId String [note: 'If the holder is also a points member.']
  lcpPartyLedgerId String
  lcpIssuedOn DateTime [not null]
  lcpValidFrom DateTime
  lcpValidUpto DateTime
  lcpSrcModule String
  lcpSrcDocType String
  lcpSrcDocId String
  lcpSrcAccYear String
  lcpSrcDocRefno String
  lcpFaceValue Decimal [not null, note: 'Snapshot at issue, and must be POSITIVE (ck_lcp_face) — so an uncapped
PERCENT batch cannot issue a coupon. Give such a batch an lcbMaxValue and
snapshot that as the face.']
  lcpIssueAmount Decimal [not null, default: 0, note: 'What was actually PAID for it: 0 for a giveaway, face value for a sold
card, something between for a discounted promotion. Breakage — vouchers
sold and never spent — has no other source.']
  lcpUsedValue Decimal [not null, default: 0]
  lcpTopupValue Decimal [not null, default: 0]
  lcpUseCount Int [not null, default: 0]
  lcpLastUsedOn DateTime [note: 'MAX(lctTxnDate) over this coupon\'s movements, recomputed with the value
buckets. Added by this migration and not by the design note, whose
recompute contract writes it against a column it never declared.']
  lcpBalanceValue Decimal [note: 'GENERATED ALWAYS ... STORED: face + topup - used. Read-only — never send
it on a create or update, and @default(dbgenerated()) marks it DB-owned
so migrate never emits a default of its own.']
  lcpStatus String [not null, default: 'ISSUED', note: 'ISSUED | PARTIAL | REDEEMED | EXPIRED | CANCELLED | BLOCKED.

Deliberately NOT a generated column. The first three are arithmetic, but
EXPIRED, CANCELLED and BLOCKED are DECISIONS and cannot be derived from
the amounts — and expiry additionally depends on today\'s date, which a
generated column may not read. recomputeCoupons() moves it between the
first three ONLY; the admin path and couponExpiryRun() own the rest.
Writing a decision from a recompute is how a cancelled voucher silently
comes back to life.']
  lcpRedeemedOn DateTime
  lcpCancelledOn DateTime
  lcpCancelledBy String
  lcpCancelReason String
  lcpRemarks String
  lcpIsActive Boolean [not null, default: true]
  lcpIsDeleted Boolean [not null, default: false]
  lcpSyncDate DateTime
  lcpCreatedOn DateTime [default: `now()`, not null]
  lcpCreatedBy String
  lcpModifiedOn DateTime
  lcpModifiedBy String
  batch loyalty_coupon_batch [not null]
  company companys [not null]
  branch branch_master
  customer customers
  member loyalty_member
  cancelledByUser user_master
  partyLedger acc_ledger_master
  txns loyalty_coupon_txn [not null]
  promotionUsages promotion_usage [not null]

  Note: 'One ISSUED coupon.

NOT partitioned, and never carried forward at year end. A voucher issued in
2025-2026 and spent in 2027-2028 is the SAME row throughout — which is what
makes LoyaltyCouponTxn\'s reference to it a plain foreign key. lcpAccYear is
the FY of ISSUE and a reporting filter only, never a partition key.

lcpFaceValue is a SNAPSHOT taken at issue. Re-pricing a batch must not
re-price the cards already in customers\' wallets — the same reason
AccTenderDetail snapshots its ledger and its rate.'
}

Table loyalty_coupon_batch {
  lcbId String [pk]
  lcbCompId String [not null]
  lcbBranchId String [note: 'NULL = every branch.']
  lcbTenantId String
  lcbCode String [not null, note: 'Stable handle, never renamed — retire the batch instead.']
  lcbName String [not null]
  lcbSource String [not null, note: 'SOLD | CAMPAIGN']
  lcbValueType String [not null, default: 'AMOUNT', note: 'AMOUNT | PERCENT']
  lcbFaceValue Decimal [not null, default: 0]
  lcbPercent Decimal [not null, default: 0]
  lcbMaxValue Decimal [note: 'Cap on a PERCENT coupon, and the only case where a cap means anything
(ck_lcb_cap_percent). An uncapped PERCENT batch cannot issue a coupon,
because ck_lcp_face demands a positive face value to snapshot — so give
a PERCENT batch a cap and use it as the face.']
  lcbMinBillAmount Decimal [not null, default: 0]
  lcbAllowPartial Boolean [not null, default: false, note: 'May a 500 voucher go 300 now and 200 next week. This is what decides
whether lcpUsedValue behaves as a balance or as a switch.']
  lcbAllowTopup Boolean [not null, default: false, note: 'Reloadable gift card. SOLD only.']
  lcbMaxPerBill Int [not null, default: 1]
  lcbIsTransferable Boolean [not null, default: true, note: 'false = bound to lcpCustId.']
  lcbPinRequired Boolean [not null, default: false]
  lcbPrefix String
  lcbCodeLength Int [not null, default: 12]
  lcbMaxIssueCount Int [note: 'NULL = unlimited. Checked by the service WHEN IT ISSUES, not by the
recompute — a recompute reports, it does not refuse.']
  lcbIssuedCount Int [not null, default: 0, note: 'Caches of LoyaltyCoupon, recomputed by
LoyaltyCouponService.recomputeCouponBatches(). There is no trigger; no
other code may write these.']
  lcbRedeemedCount Int [not null, default: 0]
  lcbValidFrom DateTime
  lcbValidUpto DateTime
  lcbValidityDays Int [note: 'SOLD vouchers usually expire N days after THEIR OWN issue rather than on
a batch-wide date; this is that N.']
  lcbBranchScope String [not null, default: 'ALL', note: 'ALL | LIST']
  lcbCustGroupId String
  lcbRedeemTenderId String [note: 'The VOUCHER tender (type 11) redemption settles through. NULL = the
branch\'s default. The rate and the floor stay owned by the tender
master, exactly as they do for points.']
  lcbRemarks String
  lcbIsActive Boolean [not null, default: true]
  lcbIsDeleted Boolean [not null, default: false]
  lcbSyncDate DateTime
  lcbCreatedOn DateTime [default: `now()`, not null]
  lcbCreatedBy String
  lcbModifiedOn DateTime
  lcbModifiedBy String
  company companys [not null]
  branch branch_master
  redeemTender acc_tender_master
  custGroup cust_groups
  coupons loyalty_coupon [not null]
  couponTxns loyalty_coupon_txn [not null]

  Note: 'The coupon batch — what a coupon of this kind is worth and how it behaves.

A coupon is a MONEY instrument with a code on it. It is not points, it
shares no ledger with points, and it redeems through its own tender type
(accounts.acc_tender_types 11, VOUCHER).

Two sources, and only two:
SOLD      a gift voucher the customer bought. May be RELOADED
(lcbAllowTopup), which is what makes it a gift CARD rather
than a one-shot slip.
CAMPAIGN  codes issued in bulk and handed out or mailed. Not reloadable
(ck_lcb_topup) — a campaign code is not a wallet.

A PERCENT batch ("20% off, up to 500") resolves against the bill at the
moment of redemption and only the RESOLVED RUPEE AMOUNT reaches
lctAmount and acc_tender_detail.td_amount. The percentage never leaves
this row.'
}

Table loyalty_coupon_txn {
  lctId String [not null]
  lctCompId String [not null]
  lctBranchId String [not null, note: 'Where it was presented. NOT NULL — the expiry sweep must therefore fall
back to the batch\'s branch when the voucher has none.']
  lctTenantId String
  lctAccYear String [not null]
  lctLcpId String [not null]
  lctLcbId String [not null, note: 'Snapshot, so batch reports need no hop through the coupon.']
  lctCustId String
  lctMemberId String
  lctTxnType String [not null, note: 'REDEEM | TOPUP | CANCEL | EXPIRE']
  lctRowNo Int [not null, default: 1]
  lctAmount Decimal [not null, note: 'Positive unless it is a reversal, whatever the type (ck_lct_sign).']
  lctTxnDate DateTime [not null]
  lctBillAmount Decimal [not null, default: 0, note: 'For a PERCENT batch: the bill it was measured against, and the rate that
produced lctAmount. Without these the resolved figure is unexplainable
afterwards.']
  lctPercentApplied Decimal [not null, default: 0]
  lctSrcModule String
  lctSrcDocType String
  lctSrcDocId String
  lctSrcAccYear String
  lctSrcDocRefno String
  lctTenderId String [note: 'The tender line (type 11, VOUCHER) the money went out on. Required on a
REDEEM (ck_lct_redeem_tender) and forbidden on EXPIRE or CANCEL
(ck_lct_no_tender) — nothing was paid out. This is also the only route by
which a redemption reaches the GL and then Tally.']
  lctTenderAccYear String
  lctReversalOfId String
  lctReversalOfAccYear String
  lctReversalReason String
  lctApprovedBy String [note: 'Voiding a live voucher is somebody\'s decision — ck_lct_cancel_approval
makes CANCEL require this.']
  lctUserId String
  lctSessionId String
  lctDeviceId String
  lctRemarks String
  lctIsDeleted Boolean [not null, default: false]
  lctSyncDate DateTime
  lctCreatedOn DateTime [default: `now()`, not null]
  lctCreatedBy String
  lctModifiedOn DateTime
  lctModifiedBy String
  coupon loyalty_coupon [not null]
  batch loyalty_coupon_batch [not null]
  customer customers
  member loyalty_member
  company companys [not null]
  branch branch_master [not null]
  user user_master
  approver user_master
  device device_master
  tender acc_tender_detail
  reversalOf loyalty_coupon_txn
  reversedBy loyalty_coupon_txn [not null]

  indexes {
    (lctId, lctAccYear) [pk]
  }

  Note: 'One movement against one coupon.

LIST-partitioned by lctAccYear — the year of the MOVEMENT, not of the
issue: a 2027 spend of a 2025 voucher lands in 2027 and the voucher row
never moves. Hence the composite PK. Partitions
(sales.loyalty_coupon_txn_2026_2027) come from
public.ensure_acc_year_partitions(\'YYYY-YYYY\'), which
20260921160000_add_coupon_voucher_engine taught about this table.

lctAmount is SIGNED and always means "what this event does to the
voucher": REDEEM / EXPIRE / CANCEL consume it, TOPUP adds to it (into its
own bucket). A reversal repeats its TYPE with the opposite sign and names
the row it undoes — there is no REVERSAL type, so each bucket needs one
summing rule rather than two.

BEWARE, because it is the opposite of the points ledger and the two live in
the same schema: HERE a spend is POSITIVE and the type carries the
direction. In LoyaltyLedger a spend is NEGATIVE. Do not copy one recompute
into the other.

WRITE RULE: exactly one private method of LoyaltyCouponService may write
this table. It writes the rows, then recomputes the touched COUPONS and
then their BATCHES, in that order, before returning — lcbRedeemedCount
counts vouchers by STATUS, so a batch re-counted first counts a status
that is about to change.'
}

Table loyalty_gift_redeem {
  lgrId String [not null]
  lgrCompId String [not null]
  lgrBranchId String [not null]
  lgrTenantId String
  lgrAccYear String [not null]
  lgrDeviceId String
  lgrRedeemSlno BigInt [not null, note: 'Raw per-device serial — no server round trip needed to allocate one.']
  lgrRedeemRefno String [not null, note: 'The printed number, which embeds the counter series.']
  lgrRedeemDate DateTime [not null]
  lgrRedeemTime DateTime
  lgrMemberId String [not null]
  lgrCustId String [not null]
  lgrLscId String [note: 'Scheme it was claimed under.']
  lgrUserId String [note: 'The login that saved it.']
  lgrEmpId String [note: 'The person who served, if different from the login.']
  lgrOpeningPoints Decimal [not null, default: 0]
  lgrRedeemPoints Decimal [not null, default: 0]
  lgrClosingPoints Decimal [not null, default: 0]
  lgrTotalQty Decimal [not null, default: 0]
  lgrTotalValue Decimal [not null, default: 0, note: 'At MRP / issue rate.']
  lgrTotalCost Decimal [not null, default: 0, note: 'At cost, for the giveaway P&L.']
  lgrOtpNo String [note: 'One-time code that authorised the redemption. Points are bearer value;
a counter able to spend them unchallenged is a fraud route.']
  lgrApprovedBy String
  lgrStatus String [not null, default: 'DRAFT', note: 'DRAFT | CONFIRMED | CANCELLED. A cancelled slip must name who authorised
the cancellation (ck_lgr_cancel), and may never carry a live voucher.']
  lgrPrintCount Int [not null, default: 0]
  lgrPostedVoucherId String [note: 'The AccVoucherHeader this slip posted to, once the server has posted it.

NO FOREIGN KEY, deliberately, and for the same reason
sale_bill.sb_posted_voucher_id has none: this is a counter document
created OFFLINE, so the row exists long before the voucher does. An FK
would make the counter\'s insert depend on a server-side write it cannot
see. The accounts-side documents (AccBillBalance, AccPdcRegister) DO
carry the FK, because they are only ever written server-side after
posting — a different lifecycle, a different rule.

This is the ONLY route by which a gift redemption reaches Tally: export
happens at the voucher layer, never from a source document. The
accounting year travels with the id so the lookup hits one partition.']
  lgrVoucherAccYear String
  lgrReversalOfId String
  lgrReversalOfAccYear String
  lgrReversalReason String
  lgrRemarks String
  lgrIsDeleted Boolean [not null, default: false]
  lgrSyncDate DateTime
  lgrCreatedOn DateTime [default: `now()`, not null]
  lgrCreatedBy String
  lgrModifiedOn DateTime
  lgrModifiedBy String
  company companys [not null]
  branch branch_master [not null]
  device device_master
  member loyalty_member [not null]
  customer customers [not null]
  scheme loyalty_scheme
  user user_master
  employee employee_master
  approver user_master
  reversalOf loyalty_gift_redeem
  reversedBy loyalty_gift_redeem [not null]
  items loyalty_gift_redeem_item [not null]

  indexes {
    (lgrId, lgrAccYear) [pk]
  }

  Note: 'A gift redemption slip — A DOCUMENT IN ITS OWN RIGHT.

A customer walks up with points and walks away with goods and a printed
slip. No money crosses the counter, so this cannot be an AccTenderDetail
row. And it is not a line on a sale bill either: it has its own number
series, its own status, its own print count, and frequently no bill at all.

Shape follows SaleBill: a partitioned header with an offline-safe number
pair (a per-device serial plus the printed refno that embeds it), and a
partitioned detail carrying the stock movement.

The POINTS side is not stored here. LoyaltyLedger holds the GIFT rows — one
per lot consumed, named by lldSrcDocId — and the wallet balance follows
from them. lgrOpeningPoints / lgrRedeemPoints / lgrClosingPoints are
SNAPSHOTS for the printed slip; the ledger remains the authority.'
}

Table loyalty_gift_redeem_item {
  lgdId String [not null]
  lgdCompId String [not null]
  lgdBranchId String [not null]
  lgdTenantId String
  lgdAccYear String [not null]
  lgdLgrId String [not null]
  lgdLgrAccYear String [not null]
  lgdRowNo Int [not null, default: 1]
  lgdLsgId String [note: 'The catalogue row claimed, when the gift came from a scheme\'s list.']
  lgdItemId String [not null]
  lgdUnitId String [not null, note: 'item_unit_conversion, as on a bill line.']
  lgdGodownId String
  lgdBarcode String
  lgdBatchId String
  lgdBatchNo String
  lgdSerialNo String
  lgdBatchDate DateTime
  lgdExpiryDate DateTime
  lgdQty Decimal [not null, note: 'No database default: ck_lgd_qty demands it be positive, so a row that
omits it must fail rather than silently issue nothing.']
  lgdStockQty Decimal [not null, default: 0, note: 'On hand at the moment of issue.']
  lgdRate Decimal [not null, default: 0, note: 'Issue rate.']
  lgdTotal Decimal [not null, default: 0]
  lgdPoints Decimal [not null, default: 0, note: 'Points this line cost.']
  lgdMaxPrice Decimal [not null, default: 0]
  lgdCostPrice Decimal [not null, default: 0, note: 'The giveaway\'s cost — what a Tally Stock Journal posts against.']
  lgdCostWot Decimal [not null, default: 0, note: 'Cost excluding tax.']
  lgdNotes String
  lgdIsDeleted Boolean [not null, default: false]
  lgdSyncDate DateTime
  lgdCreatedOn DateTime [default: `now()`, not null]
  lgdCreatedBy String
  lgdModifiedOn DateTime
  lgdModifiedBy String
  header loyalty_gift_redeem [not null]
  company companys [not null]
  branch branch_master [not null]
  gift loyalty_scheme_gift
  item item_master [not null]
  unit item_unit_conversion [not null]
  godown godown_locations
  batch item_batch_master

  indexes {
    (lgdId, lgdAccYear) [pk]
  }

  Note: 'The goods issued against a redemption slip.

ONE detail table, not two. The legacy pair (loy_item_rdm_items and
loy_item_rdm_itm_det) held identical column sets against the same header
and only one was live, so only one is rebuilt.

Carries the full stock movement — godown, batch, serial, expiry — because a
gift relieves inventory exactly as a sale does, and a batch-tracked shop
cannot post the issue without them. Cost is snapshotted alongside the issue
rate so the giveaway can be valued afterwards without re-pricing history.

Partitioned on its own lgdAccYear, which ck_lgd_same_year pins to the
header\'s — the composite FK requires it and the CHECK makes it readable.'
}

Table loyalty_ledger {
  lldId String [not null]
  lldCompId String [not null]
  lldBranchId String [not null, note: 'WHERE THE EVENT HAPPENED. Points earned at one branch and spent at
another are the normal case for a chain; this column is what makes
"who gave them, who honoured them" answerable, and it is the whole basis
of inter-branch settlement between franchisees.']
  lldTenantId String
  lldAccYear String [not null, note: 'FY of the EVENT, and the partition key.']
  lldMemberId String [not null]
  lldCustId String [not null, note: 'Snapshot; every report groups by it.']
  lldLscId String
  lldLssId String
  lldLsiId String
  lldTxnType String [not null, note: 'EARN | REDEEM | GIFT | EXPIRE | ADJUST | TRANSFER | OPENING']
  lldRowNo Int [not null, default: 1]
  lldPoints Decimal [not null, note: 'SIGNED. EARN/OPENING positive, REDEEM/GIFT/EXPIRE negative,
ADJUST/TRANSFER either. A reversal repeats the type with the opposite
sign (ck_lld_sign enforces the pairing).']
  lldTxnDate DateTime [not null]
  lldTxnTime DateTime [note: 'Which side of a happy-hour boundary the till was on. Without it, a
double-points window is unauditable the morning after.']
  lldLotId String [note: 'On a consuming row: the EARN row these points came out of. Travels as a
pair because this table is partitioned.']
  lldLotAccYear String
  lldConsumedPoints Decimal [not null, default: 0, note: 'On the LOT row only: how much of it has been spent, expired or moved
away. Recomputed by LoyaltyLedgerService.recomputeLots(); there is no
trigger and no other code may write it.']
  lldLotBalance Decimal [note: 'GENERATED ALWAYS ... STORED: lldPoints - lldConsumedPoints on a lot row,
ZERO (not NULL) on a consuming row — which is what keeps the "> 0" index
predicates cheap and total. Read-only: never send it on a create or
update, and @default(dbgenerated()) keeps migrate from emitting a default.']
  lldExpiresOn DateTime [note: 'When this lot lapses, from lsc_points_valid_days / lsc_expiry_basis at
earn time. NULL = never expires, and such lots are consumed LAST.']
  lldActiveFrom DateTime [note: 'When this lot becomes REDEEMABLE: bill date + lsc_activation_days. Before
it, the points exist and show in the balance but cannot be spent — the
return-window defence against clawing back points already spent.
NULL = redeemable at once.']
  lldSrcModule String
  lldSrcDocType String
  lldSrcDocId String
  lldSrcAccYear String [note: 'The SOURCE\'s FY, not this row\'s.']
  lldSrcDocRefno String [note: 'Snapshot, so a member statement prints the bill number without joining a
partitioned table across every year the customer has traded.']
  lldSrcRowNo Int
  lldBaseAmount Decimal [not null, default: 0]
  lldBaseQty Decimal [not null, default: 0]
  lldRate Decimal [not null, default: 0, note: 'Points per slab on an earn, or INR per point on a redeem. Snapshotted so
a historic redemption stays re-derivable whichever of
lsc_redeem_value_per_point or tnd_conversion_rate was in play.']
  lldFactor Decimal [not null, default: 1]
  lldMoneyValue Decimal [not null, default: 0, note: 'What they were worth, in INR.']
  lldTenderId String [note: 'Redemption is a TENDER LINE: AccTenderDetail with td_tender_type_id = 10
(LOYALTY), td_units_used = the points, td_conversion_rate = rupees per
point. This pair is what ties the two sides together, and it is the ONLY
route by which a redemption reaches the GL and then Tally — earning posts
nothing at all, being statistical.']
  lldTenderAccYear String
  lldReversalOfId String [note: 'Carries a real FK, unlike abj_reversal_of_id, because this table has the
year to complete the composite key with.']
  lldReversalOfAccYear String
  lldReversalReason String
  lldApprovedBy String [note: 'A manual adjustment is somebody\'s decision, not a keying accident —
ck_lld_adjust_approval makes ADJUST require this.']
  lldUserId String
  lldSessionId String
  lldDeviceId String
  lldRemarks String
  lldIsDeleted Boolean [not null, default: false]
  lldSyncDate DateTime
  lldCreatedOn DateTime [default: `now()`, not null]
  lldCreatedBy String
  lldModifiedOn DateTime
  lldModifiedBy String
  member loyalty_member [not null]
  customer customers [not null]
  company companys [not null]
  branch branch_master [not null]
  scheme loyalty_scheme
  slab loyalty_scheme_slab
  itemRule loyalty_scheme_item
  user user_master
  approver user_master
  device device_master
  lot loyalty_ledger
  lotConsumers loyalty_ledger [not null]
  reversalOf loyalty_ledger
  reversedBy loyalty_ledger [not null]
  tender acc_tender_detail

  indexes {
    (lldId, lldAccYear) [pk]
  }

  Note: 'One row per point movement against one wallet, drawn from ONE lot.

LIST-partitioned by lldAccYear — the year the EVENT happened, not the year
the points were earned. That is the whole trick: July\'s redemption of
April\'s points lands in July\'s partition and April\'s earn row never moves.
Hence the composite PK (lldId, lldAccYear). Partitions
(sales.loyalty_ledger_2026_2027) come from
public.ensure_acc_year_partitions(\'YYYY-YYYY\'), which
20260921140000_add_loyalty_points_engine taught about this table.

A row is never edited and never deleted. Everything is undone by a NEW row
of opposite sign naming the row it undoes (lldReversalOfId) — so a plain
SUM per txn type nets reversals out and no caller special-cases them.

EARN rows ARE the lots. They carry lldExpiresOn, lldActiveFrom and
lldConsumedPoints; lldLotBalance is what is left. Consuming rows
(REDEEM / GIFT / EXPIRE) carry lldLotId pointing back at the lot they came
out of, so a redemption spanning three lots is THREE rows sharing ONE
tender. It costs rows and buys expiry that is exact and reversible.

FIFO, defined in ONE place (LoyaltyLedgerService.lots()) and nowhere else:
ORDER BY lld_expires_on NULLS LAST, lld_txn_date, lld_id
Oldest expiry first; within one expiry date, oldest earned first; lots that
never expire last of all — the customer-favourable order, because it spends
what would otherwise lapse.

WRITE RULE: exactly one private method of LoyaltyLedgerService may INSERT,
UPDATE or soft-delete this table. It writes the rows, then recomputes the
touched LOTS and then the touched MEMBERS, in that order, before returning.
Nothing else touches it — not a repository, not the sync handler, not a
migration script. There is no trigger to fall back on.'
}

Table loyalty_member {
  lmbId String [pk]
  lmbCompId String [not null]
  lmbBranchId String [note: 'Branch that ENROLLED them. Never a scope — a wallet belongs to the company.']
  lmbTenantId String
  lmbAccYear String [not null, note: 'FY of enrolment. Reporting filter only, never a partition key.']
  lmbCustId String [not null]
  lmbPartyLedgerId String [note: 'The accounting party, snapshotted so a points-liability report can join
accounts without a hop through the customer master. Nullable: a cash
customer may have no ledger row yet.']
  lmbLscId String
  lmbCardNo String
  lmbMobile String [note: 'Snapshot of customers.cus_phone1 — the supermarket lookup handle.']
  lmbEnrolledOn DateTime [not null]
  lmbEarnedPoints Decimal [not null, default: 0]
  lmbRedeemedPoints Decimal [not null, default: 0]
  lmbExpiredPoints Decimal [not null, default: 0]
  lmbGiftPoints Decimal [not null, default: 0]
  lmbAdjustedPoints Decimal [not null, default: 0]
  lmbBalancePoints Decimal [note: 'GENERATED ALWAYS ... STORED in the DB:
earned + adjusted - redeemed - expired - gift
Read-only: never send it on a create or update — Postgres rejects it, and
@default(dbgenerated()) marks it DB-owned so migrate never emits a
default of its own.

This is what the bill screen shows. It is NOT what the customer may spend
today: cooling lots and unswept lapsed lots make those differ, and
LoyaltyLedgerService.redeemable() answers that instead.']
  lmbLifetimeBillAmt Decimal [not null, default: 0, note: 'Lifetime trading, which points alone cannot answer: lmbEarnedPoints is
already "everything ever earned" (redemption never touches that bucket),
so a lifetime-points column would just be a second copy of it. These two
are not derivable from the point columns at all.']
  lmbLifetimeBillCnt Int [not null, default: 0]
  lmbLastEarnOn DateTime
  lmbLastRedeemOn DateTime
  lmbLastActivityOn DateTime [note: 'The dormant-member report and lsc_expiry_basis = \'LAST_TXN\' have no other
source once the row simply stays open across years.']
  lmbNextExpiryOn DateTime [note: 'Earliest open lot, so the till can say "480 points expire in 9 days"
without a scan of the ledger. Recomputed AFTER the lots — it reads
lldLotBalance, so the other order publishes a wrong date silently.']
  lmbStatus String [not null, default: 'ACTIVE', note: 'ACTIVE | SUSPENDED | CLOSED | MERGED. MERGED is two wallets that turn out
to be one person: the loser is marked MERGED and its points move by a
PAIR of ADJUST ledger rows — never by editing a balance, which would
leave the trail with a hole in it.']
  lmbMergedIntoId String
  lmbBlockReason String
  lmbRemarks String
  lmbIsActive Boolean [not null, default: true]
  lmbIsDeleted Boolean [not null, default: false]
  lmbSyncDate DateTime
  lmbCreatedOn DateTime [default: `now()`, not null]
  lmbCreatedBy String
  lmbModifiedOn DateTime
  lmbModifiedBy String
  company companys [not null]
  branch branch_master
  customer customers [not null]
  partyLedger acc_ledger_master
  scheme loyalty_scheme
  mergedInto loyalty_member
  mergedFrom loyalty_member [not null]
  ledgerRows loyalty_ledger [not null]
  giftRedeems loyalty_gift_redeem [not null]
  coupons loyalty_coupon [not null]
  couponTxns loyalty_coupon_txn [not null]

  Note: 'The points wallet: ONE per (company, customer).

NOT partitioned, and never carried forward at year end — this is
AccBillBalance applied to points. A customer who earned in 2025-2026 and
spends in 2027-2028 is the SAME row throughout, which is precisely what
makes LoyaltyLedger\'s reference to it a plain foreign key instead of a
composite one that has to carry a year around.

lmbAccYear records the year they ENROLLED and is a reporting filter only.
Making it a partition key is exactly the mistake the bill-balance
repartitioning exists to undo.

Keyed by company and customer, never by branch: that single index
(ux_lmb_cust) is the chain-store guarantee that points earned at one branch
spend at another. lmbCardNo and lmbMobile are ALTERNATE HANDLES for finding
this row, not identities — the identity is lmbCustId.'
}

Table loyalty_scheme {
  lscId String [pk]
  lscCompId String [not null]
  lscBranchId String [note: 'Single-shop shorthand. NULL = every branch.']
  lscTenantId String
  lscCode String [not null]
  lscName String [not null]
  lscType String [not null, default: 'BOTH']
  lscStatus String [not null, default: 'DRAFT']
  lscPriority Int [not null, default: 1, note: 'Which scheme wins when several match one bill. 1 = primary.']
  lscAutoApply Boolean [not null, default: true]
  lscApplyOn String [not null, default: 'BILL_AMOUNT']
  lscCalcOnAmountType String [not null, default: 'NET_AMOUNT']
  lscIncludeTax Boolean [not null, default: false]
  lscBillType String [not null, default: 'ALL']
  lscMinBillAmount Decimal [not null, default: 0]
  lscMaxEarnPoints Decimal [not null, default: 0, note: 'Per bill. 0 = uncapped.']
  lscEarnOnDiscounted Boolean [not null, default: true]
  lscEarnOnCharges Boolean [not null, default: false]
  lscEarnWithRedeem Boolean [not null, default: false]
  lscRoundingMethod String [not null, default: 'FLOOR']
  lscPointsDecimals Int [not null, default: 2]
  lscBranchScope String [not null, default: 'ALL']
  lscCustScope String [not null, default: 'ALL']
  lscItemScope String [not null, default: 'ALL']
  lscPriceLevelId Int
  lscPoolMode String [not null, default: 'COMPANY', note: 'COMPANY = one wallet, earned anywhere, spent anywhere (the default, and
what a chain wants). BRANCH = a franchisee honours only what it issued.']
  lscAllowCrossBranchRedeem Boolean [not null, default: true]
  lscAllowPointRedeem Boolean [not null, default: false]
  lscAllowGiftRedeem Boolean [not null, default: false]
  lscRedeemTenderId String [note: 'accounts.acc_tender_master(tnd_id) — the LOYALTY tender (type 10).']
  lscRedeemValuePerPoint Decimal [not null, default: 0, note: 'Rupees per point. WINS over acc_tender_master.tnd_conversion_rate whenever
a scheme matches; the tender master is the fallback for a company running
no scheme, and acc_tender_detail.td_conversion_rate snapshots whichever was
used. A write-path rule the schema cannot enforce.']
  lscMinRedeemPoints Decimal [not null, default: 0]
  lscMaxRedeemPoints Decimal [not null, default: 0, note: 'Per bill. 0 = uncapped.']
  lscMaxRedeemPerc Decimal [not null, default: 100, note: 'Per bill: the % of it points may settle.']
  lscRedeemMinBillAmount Decimal [not null, default: 0]
  lscRedeemMultiple Decimal [not null, default: 0, note: '0 = any quantity.']
  lscExpiryBasis String [not null, default: 'EARN_DATE']
  lscPointsValidDays Int [not null, default: 0]
  lscActivationDays Int [not null, default: 0, note: 'THE CLAW-BACK DEFENCE. Points earn on bill save (offline included) but are
not REDEEMABLE until this many days have passed — long enough for the
return window to close. 0 = redeemable immediately.']
  lscReturnMode String [not null, default: 'REVERSE']
  lscStartDate DateTime [not null]
  lscEndDate DateTime [not null]
  lscValidFromTime DateTime [note: 'Both bounds or neither. from > to legitimately means "spans midnight" —
22:22 to 04:44 is a late-night promotion, not a typo.']
  lscValidToTime DateTime
  lscValidWeekdays String [note: '\'MON,TUE,...\'; NULL = every day.']
  lscRemarks String
  lscIsActive Boolean [not null, default: true]
  lscIsDeleted Boolean [not null, default: false]
  lscSyncDate DateTime
  lscCreatedOn DateTime [default: `now()`, not null]
  lscCreatedBy String
  lscModifiedOn DateTime
  lscModifiedBy String
  lscApprovedOn DateTime
  lscApprovedBy String
  company companys [not null]
  branch branch_master
  priceLevel item_price_levels
  redeemTender acc_tender_master
  approvedBy user_master
  branches loyalty_scheme_branch [not null]
  parties loyalty_scheme_party [not null]
  items loyalty_scheme_item [not null]
  slabs loyalty_scheme_slab [not null]
  gifts loyalty_scheme_gift [not null]
  loyaltyMembers loyalty_member [not null]
  loyaltyLedgerRows loyalty_ledger [not null]
  loyaltyGiftRedeems loyalty_gift_redeem [not null]

  Note: 'sales.loyalty_scheme — §1, the loyalty campaign header.

The RULES: who earns, on what, how fast, and what a point is worth. It holds
no balances and no movements — those belong to the points ledger (13) and the
coupon/gift tables (14), neither of which is in this schema yet.

Supersedes sales.loyalty_sch_list / _party / _points / _gift, which
20260831060000_replace_loyalty_scheme_tables drops.

Declared here WITHOUT its CHECK constraints expressed in Prisma — Prisma has
no concept of them. The migration DOES create them, and
utils/loyalty-scheme-invariants.ts mirrors every one so a rejected campaign
comes back as a field-addressed 400 rather than a Postgres error string the
screen cannot place on an input.

DB-only objects the migration hand-authors, none of them expressible here:
ux_lsc_code       UNIQUE (lsc_comp_id, lower(lsc_code)) WHERE NOT deleted
ux_lsc_primary    UNIQUE (comp, branch, type) NULLS NOT DISTINCT
WHERE priority = 1 AND APPROVED AND active AND NOT deleted
ix_lsc_resolve    the per-bill resolver, covering, partial
ix_lsc_branch / ix_lsc_price_level / ix_lsc_redeem_tender /
ix_lsc_approved_by   FK-covering, all partial'
}

Table loyalty_scheme_branch {
  lsbId String [pk]
  lsbLscId String [not null]
  lsbSlno Int [not null, default: 1]
  lsbBranchId String [not null]
  lsbIsExclude Boolean [not null, default: false]
  lsbNotes String
  lsbIsActive Boolean [not null, default: true]
  lsbIsDeleted Boolean [not null, default: false]
  lsbSyncDate DateTime
  lsbCreatedOn DateTime [default: `now()`, not null]
  lsbCreatedBy String
  lsbModifiedOn DateTime
  lsbModifiedBy String
  scheme loyalty_scheme [not null]
  branch branch_master [not null]

  Note: 'sales.loyalty_scheme_branch — §2, which branches run the campaign.

Read only when lsc_branch_scope = \'LIST\'. lsbIsExclude makes "every branch
except two" two rows rather than thirty-eight; an EXCLUDE row always beats an
INCLUDE row for the same branch.

DB-only objects the migration hand-authors:
ux_lsb_row     UNIQUE (lsb_lsc_id, lsb_branch_id) WHERE NOT deleted
ix_lsb_scheme  (lsb_lsc_id), partial
ix_lsb_branch  (lsb_branch_id), partial'
}

Table loyalty_scheme_gift {
  lsgId String [pk]
  lsgLscId String [not null]
  lsgSlno Int [not null, default: 1]
  lsgItemId String [not null]
  lsgUnitId String [not null]
  lsgItemQty Decimal [not null, default: 1]
  lsgRedeemPoints Decimal [not null, default: 0]
  lsgRepeat Boolean [not null, default: false, note: 'Claimable more than once.']
  lsgMaxQtyPerBill Decimal [not null, default: 0, note: '0 = uncapped.']
  lsgStockCheck Boolean [not null, default: true, note: 'Block the claim when the item is not in stock at the issuing branch. A
gift the shop cannot hand over is worse than one it never offered.']
  lsgValidFrom DateTime
  lsgValidUpto DateTime
  lsgNotes String
  lsgIsActive Boolean [not null, default: true]
  lsgIsDeleted Boolean [not null, default: false]
  lsgSyncDate DateTime
  lsgCreatedOn DateTime [default: `now()`, not null]
  lsgCreatedBy String
  lsgModifiedOn DateTime
  lsgModifiedBy String
  scheme loyalty_scheme [not null]
  item item_master [not null]
  unit item_unit_conversion [not null]
  loyaltyGiftItems loyalty_gift_redeem_item [not null]

  Note: 'sales.loyalty_scheme_gift — §6, points buy an ITEM.

The CATALOGUE of what MAY be claimed. What was actually handed over belongs
in sales.loyalty_gift_issue (13), which is not in this schema yet.

A gift redemption is NOT a tender: no money crosses the counter, stock does.
lsgUnitId references inventory.item_unit_conversion(iuc_id) — the same target
sale_bill_item.fk_sbi_item_unit uses — because the gift becomes a bill line
and the two must agree without a conversion lookup.

DB-only objects to hand-author:
ux_lsg_item    UNIQUE (lsc_id, item_id, unit_id) WHERE NOT deleted
ix_lsg_scheme  (lsc_id, redeem_points) INCLUDE (...), partial
ix_lsg_item / ix_lsg_unit, both partial'
}

Table loyalty_scheme_item {
  lsiId String [pk]
  lsiLscId String [not null]
  lsiSlno Int [not null, default: 1]
  lsiKind String [not null]
  lsiScopeId String [not null]
  lsiItemId String
  lsiGroupId String
  lsiCategoryId String
  lsiBrandId String
  lsiSectionId String
  lsiIsExclude Boolean [not null, default: false]
  lsiFactor Decimal [not null, default: 1, note: 'Multiplies whatever the slab computed for the line. 2.0 = double points.']
  lsiPoints Decimal [not null, default: 0, note: 'Non-zero REPLACES the slab result outright instead of scaling it.']
  lsiMaxPoints Decimal [not null, default: 0, note: '0 = uncapped, per line.']
  lsiMatchPriority Int [not null, default: 1]
  lsiNotes String
  lsiIsActive Boolean [not null, default: true]
  lsiIsDeleted Boolean [not null, default: false]
  lsiSyncDate DateTime
  lsiCreatedOn DateTime [default: `now()`, not null]
  lsiCreatedBy String
  lsiModifiedOn DateTime
  lsiModifiedBy String
  scheme loyalty_scheme [not null]
  item item_master
  itemGroup item_group_master
  itemCategory item_category_master
  itemBrand item_brand_master
  itemSection item_section_master
  loyaltyLedgerRows loyalty_ledger [not null]

  Note: 'sales.loyalty_scheme_item — §4, which stock the campaign applies to.

One kind + one scope id, with five GENERATED ALWAYS ... STORED columns
carrying the foreign keys — the same shape as LoyaltySchemeParty, read that
model\'s header for the write rules and the migration caveat. In short: set
lsiKind + lsiScopeId and never touch itemId / groupId / categoryId / brandId
/ sectionId.

lsiKind = \'ITEM\' | \'ITEM_GROUP\' | \'ITEM_CATEGORY\' | \'ITEM_BRAND\' | \'ITEM_SECTION\'

The vocabulary is the live one: loyalty_sch_list.ls_item_type already used
exactly these five values, so the new screen and the old data agree.

This is what makes "double points on own-brand, but nothing on tobacco"
expressible in ONE scheme — the predecessor could hold only one kind of
grouping per scheme and could not exclude at all.

lsiFactor multiplies whatever the slab computed for the line (2.0 = double
points); a non-zero lsiPoints replaces the slab result outright instead of
scaling it.

lsiMatchPriority: most specific wins. Seed ITEM 4, ITEM_BRAND 3,
ITEM_CATEGORY 2, ITEM_SECTION 1, ITEM_GROUP 0 — data, not a hard-coded rule.
An EXCLUDE row earns nothing regardless of any broader rule.

Read only when lsc_item_scope = \'LIST\'.

DB-only objects to hand-author:
ux_lsi_row     UNIQUE (lsc_id, kind, scope_id) WHERE NOT deleted
ix_lsi_scheme  (lsc_id, match_priority DESC) INCLUDE (...), partial
ix_lsi_item / _group / _category / _brand / _section, all partial'
}

Table loyalty_scheme_party {
  lspId String [pk]
  lspLscId String [not null]
  lspSlno Int [not null, default: 1]
  lspKind String [not null]
  lspScopeId String [not null]
  lspCustId String
  lspCustGroupId String
  lspIsExclude Boolean [not null, default: false]
  lspMatchPriority Int [not null, default: 1]
  lspNotes String
  lspIsActive Boolean [not null, default: true]
  lspIsDeleted Boolean [not null, default: false]
  lspSyncDate DateTime
  lspCreatedOn DateTime [default: `now()`, not null]
  lspCreatedBy String
  lspModifiedOn DateTime
  lspModifiedBy String
  scheme loyalty_scheme [not null]
  customer customers
  customerGroup cust_groups

  Note: 'sales.loyalty_scheme_party — §3, who the campaign applies to.

ONE KIND + ONE SCOPE ID, the shape the entry grid needs. The screen binds to
exactly two columns, lspKind and lspScopeId; the two *_id fields below are
GENERATED ALWAYS ... STORED in Postgres and exist only to carry a real
foreign key per kind.

lspKind = \'CUSTOMER\' | \'CUSTOMER_GROUP\'

Loyalty deliberately has NO AREA or CITY, unlike promotion_scheme_party: a
wallet follows the person, not the route. Adding them would be one generated
column and one FK each.

lspMatchPriority makes "a rule naming one CUSTOMER beats one naming their
GROUP" data rather than an assumption: CUSTOMER 2, CUSTOMER_GROUP 1. An
EXCLUDE row beats an INCLUDE row at equal priority.

Read only when lsc_cust_scope = \'LIST\'.

── WRITING THIS MODEL ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
NEVER set custId / custGroupId, and never use their nested `connect`s.
Postgres computes those columns and rejects any write. Set lspKind +
lspScopeId; read the relations back.

── MIGRATION ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
prisma migrate cannot emit a generated column; both are hand-authored, e.g.
lsp_cust_id uuid GENERATED ALWAYS AS
(CASE WHEN lsp_kind::text = \'CUSTOMER\' THEN lsp_scope_id END) STORED
The `@default(dbgenerated())` below is the no-argument form: it stops every
later `migrate dev` emitting an ALTER COLUMN ... DROP DEFAULT that Postgres
rejects with 42601 on a generated column.

The FKs are ON UPDATE NO ACTION, not CASCADE — Postgres forbids a cascading
update on a key containing a generated column. Costs nothing: every master
key here is a uuidv7() that is never reissued.

DB-only objects to hand-author:
ux_lsp_row       UNIQUE (lsc_id, kind, scope_id) WHERE NOT deleted
ix_lsp_scheme    (lsc_id, match_priority DESC) INCLUDE (...), partial
ix_lsp_cust / ix_lsp_cust_group, both partial'
}

Table loyalty_scheme_slab {
  lssId String [pk]
  lssLscId String [not null]
  lssSlno Int [not null, default: 1]
  lssItemId String [note: 'NULL = applies to the whole bill.']
  lssUnitId String [note: 'item_unit_conversion, as on a bill line.']
  lssExceeds Decimal [not null, default: 0, note: 'Band lower bound.']
  lssUpto Decimal [note: 'Band ceiling. NULL = open-ended.']
  lssEach Decimal [not null, default: 1, note: 'Granularity.']
  lssPoints Decimal [not null, default: 0, note: 'Awarded per lssEach.']
  lssFactor Decimal [not null, default: 1]
  lssMaxPoints Decimal [not null, default: 0, note: '0 = uncapped.']
  lssNotes String
  lssIsActive Boolean [not null, default: true]
  lssIsDeleted Boolean [not null, default: false]
  lssSyncDate DateTime
  lssCreatedOn DateTime [default: `now()`, not null]
  lssCreatedBy String
  lssModifiedOn DateTime
  lssModifiedBy String
  scheme loyalty_scheme [not null]
  item item_master
  unit item_unit_conversion
  loyaltyLedgerRows loyalty_ledger [not null]

  Note: 'sales.loyalty_scheme_slab — §5, the earn rates.

One row = one rate band. Read as: for every lssEach units ABOVE lssExceeds,
award lssPoints, scaled by lssFactor.

"units" are RUPEES when lsc_apply_on is BILL_AMOUNT / ITEM_AMOUNT, and
QUANTITY when it is BILL_QTY / ITEM_QTY — one column pair serving both.

Bill-value slabs are ROWS, not columns: 0-999 -> 1pt/100, 1000-4999 ->
2pt/100, 5000+ -> 3pt/100 is three rows.

lssItemId narrows a band to ONE item; leave it NULL for a whole-bill band.
Anything broader than one item (group, brand, category) belongs in
LoyaltySchemeItem, which multiplies what this computes.

lssUnitId references inventory.item_unit_conversion(iuc_id) — the same target
sale_bill_item.fk_sbi_item_unit uses, NOT item_unit_master(unit_id).

DB-only objects to hand-author:
ux_lss_band    UNIQUE (lsc_id, COALESCE(item_id, nil uuid), exceeds)
WHERE NOT deleted
ix_lss_scheme  (lsc_id, exceeds) INCLUDE (...), partial
ix_lss_item / ix_lss_unit, both partial'
}

Table promotion_scheme {
  prmId String [pk]
  prmCompId String [not null]
  prmBranchId String
  prmTenantId String
  prmCode String [not null]
  prmName String [not null]
  prmStatus String [not null, default: 'DRAFT']
  prmApplyOn String [not null, default: 'ITEM_QTY']
  prmBenefit String [not null, default: 'DISC_PERC']
  prmPriority Int [not null, default: 1]
  prmStackMode String [not null, default: 'EXCLUSIVE']
  prmAutoApply Boolean [not null, default: true]
  prmAllowWithManualDisc Boolean [not null, default: false]
  prmCalcOnAmountType String [not null, default: 'NET_AMOUNT']
  prmIncludeTax Boolean [not null, default: false]
  prmBillType String [not null, default: 'ALL']
  prmMinBillAmount Decimal [not null, default: 0]
  prmMinQty Decimal [not null, default: 0]
  prmBranchScope String [not null, default: 'ALL']
  prmCustScope String [not null, default: 'ALL']
  prmItemScope String [not null, default: 'ALL']
  prmPriceLevelId Int
  prmMaxBenefitPerBill Decimal [not null, default: 0]
  prmMaxUsesTotal Int [not null, default: 0]
  prmMaxUsesPerCust Int [not null, default: 0]
  prmBudgetAmount Decimal [not null, default: 0]
  prmCouponBatchId String
  prmStartDate DateTime [not null]
  prmEndDate DateTime [not null]
  prmValidFromTime DateTime
  prmValidToTime DateTime
  prmValidWeekdays String
  prmRemarks String
  prmIsActive Boolean [not null, default: true]
  prmIsDeleted Boolean [not null, default: false]
  prmSyncDate DateTime
  prmCreatedOn DateTime [default: `now()`, not null]
  prmCreatedBy String
  prmModifiedOn DateTime
  prmModifiedBy String
  prmApprovedOn DateTime
  prmApprovedBy String
  company companys [not null]
  branch branch_master
  priceLevel item_price_levels
  approvedBy user_master
  branches promotion_scheme_branch [not null]
  parties promotion_scheme_party [not null]
  items promotion_scheme_item [not null]
  slabs promotion_scheme_slab [not null]
  usages promotion_usage [not null]

  indexes {
    (prmId, prmBenefit) [unique]
  }

  Note: 'sales.promotion_scheme — §1, the campaign header.

Declared here WITHOUT any CHECK constraint: the vocabulary rules
(ck_prm_status, ck_prm_apply_on, ck_prm_benefit, ck_prm_stack_mode,
ck_prm_calc_on, ck_prm_bill_type, the three *_scope checks,
ck_prm_fixed_price_scope, ck_prm_dates, ck_prm_time_pair, ck_prm_weekdays,
ck_prm_priority, ck_prm_thresholds, ck_prm_caps, ck_prm_approved) are
enforced in PromotionSchemeService, not in the migration — same treatment
item_unit_conversion already gets.

DB-only objects Prisma cannot express, to be hand-authored in the migration:
ux_prm_code      UNIQUE (prm_comp_id, lower(prm_code)) WHERE NOT deleted
ix_prm_resolve   the per-bill resolver, covering, partial on
(deleted = false AND active AND status = \'APPROVED\')
ix_prm_company / ix_prm_branch / ix_prm_price_level /
ix_prm_coupon_batch / ix_prm_approved_by   FK-covering, all partial
ex_prm_exclusive_clash   EXCLUDE USING gist — two APPROVED, EXCLUSIVE
campaigns on the same company/branch/trigger at
the same priority may not overlap in date.
Needs btree_gist.'
}

Table promotion_scheme_branch {
  prbId String [pk]
  prbPrmId String [not null]
  prbSlno Int [not null, default: 1]
  prbBranchId String [not null]
  prbIsExclude Boolean [not null, default: false]
  prbNotes String
  prbIsActive Boolean [not null, default: true]
  prbIsDeleted Boolean [not null, default: false]
  prbSyncDate DateTime
  prbCreatedOn DateTime [default: `now()`, not null]
  prbCreatedBy String
  prbModifiedOn DateTime
  prbModifiedBy String
  scheme promotion_scheme [not null]
  branch branch_master [not null]

  Note: 'sales.promotion_scheme_branch — §2, which branches run the campaign.

Read only when prm_branch_scope = \'LIST\'. prbIsExclude makes "every branch
except two" two rows rather than thirty-eight; an EXCLUDE row always beats
an INCLUDE row for the same branch.

CHECK ck_prb_slno (prb_slno > 0) is enforced in the service, not the DB.

DB-only objects to hand-author in the migration:
ux_prb_row     UNIQUE (prb_prm_id, prb_branch_id) WHERE NOT deleted
ix_prb_scheme  (prb_prm_id) INCLUDE (branch, is_exclude), partial
ix_prb_branch  (prb_branch_id), partial'
}

Table promotion_scheme_item {
  priId String [pk]
  priPrmId String [not null]
  priSlno Int [not null, default: 1]
  priKind String [not null]
  priScopeId String [not null]
  priItemId String
  priGroupId String
  priCategoryId String
  priBrandId String
  priSectionId String
  priUnitId String
  priIsExclude Boolean [not null, default: false]
  priDiscPerc Decimal [not null, default: 0]
  priDiscQty Decimal [not null, default: 0]
  priDiscAmt Decimal [not null, default: 0]
  priMinQty Decimal [not null, default: 0]
  priFactor Decimal [not null, default: 1]
  priMaxBenefit Decimal [not null, default: 0]
  priMatchPriority Int [not null, default: 1]
  priNotes String
  priIsActive Boolean [not null, default: true]
  priIsDeleted Boolean [not null, default: false]
  priSyncDate DateTime
  priCreatedOn DateTime [default: `now()`, not null]
  priCreatedBy String
  priModifiedOn DateTime
  priModifiedBy String
  scheme promotion_scheme [not null]
  item item_master
  itemGroup item_group_master
  itemCategory item_category_master
  itemBrand item_brand_master
  itemSection item_section_master
  unit item_unit_conversion

  Note: 'sales.promotion_scheme_item — §4, which stock the campaign applies to.

One kind + one scope id, with five GENERATED ALWAYS ... STORED columns
carrying the foreign keys — the same shape as PromotionSchemeParty, read
that model\'s header for the write rules and the migration caveat. In short:
set priKind + priScopeId (+ priUnitId on an ITEM row) and never touch
itemId / groupId / categoryId / brandId / sectionId.

This is what makes "20% off own-brand, but nothing on tobacco" ONE scheme.

priMatchPriority: most specific wins. Seed ITEM 4, BRAND 3, CATEGORY 2,
SECTION 1, GROUP 0 — data, not a hard-coded rule. An EXCLUDE row kills the
line regardless of any broader rule.

Read only when prm_item_scope = \'LIST\'.

── The discount trio ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
A rate may be stated here, on the item row, instead of on a slab band —
that is all "Discount On Items (Offer Per Item)" ever was. Stored in the
bill\'s own idiom because they are copied straight onto
sbi_sch_disc_perc / _qty / _amt:
priDiscPerc  percentage off the line
priDiscQty   rupees off PER UNIT
priDiscAmt   flat rupees off the line
At most one is non-zero. All three zero = this row only says "in scope".

A unit is REQUIRED on an ITEM row and FORBIDDEN on every other kind:
"10 of item X" is not a quantity until you say ten of WHAT, and a unit is a
row of item_unit_conversion keyed to one item, so on a BRAND or CATEGORY
row it would claim to apply to items that never define it.

CHECK ck_pri_kind, ck_pri_unit, ck_pri_one_rate, ck_pri_exclude,
ck_pri_values, ck_pri_slno and ck_pri_match_priority are enforced in the
service, not the DB.

DB-only objects to hand-author:
ux_pri_row     UNIQUE (prm_id, kind, scope_id) WHERE NOT deleted
ix_pri_scheme  (prm_id, match_priority DESC) INCLUDE (...), partial
ix_pri_item / _group / _category / _brand / _section / _unit, all partial'
}

Table promotion_scheme_party {
  prpId String [pk]
  prpPrmId String [not null]
  prpSlno Int [not null, default: 1]
  prpKind String [not null]
  prpScopeId String [not null]
  prpCustId String
  prpCustGroupId String
  prpAreaId String
  prpCityId String
  prpIsExclude Boolean [not null, default: false]
  prpMatchPriority Int [not null, default: 1]
  prpNotes String
  prpIsActive Boolean [not null, default: true]
  prpIsDeleted Boolean [not null, default: false]
  prpSyncDate DateTime
  prpCreatedOn DateTime [default: `now()`, not null]
  prpCreatedBy String
  prpModifiedOn DateTime
  prpModifiedBy String
  scheme promotion_scheme [not null]
  customer customers
  customerGroup cust_groups
  area area_master
  city city_master

  Note: 'sales.promotion_scheme_party — §3, who the campaign applies to.

ONE KIND + ONE SCOPE ID, the shape the entry grid needs. The screen binds
to exactly two columns, prpKind and prpScopeId; the four *_id fields below
are GENERATED ALWAYS ... STORED in Postgres and exist only to carry a real
foreign key per kind.

prpKind = \'CUSTOMER\' | \'CUSTOMER_GROUP\' | \'AREA\' | \'CITY\'

CITY reaches a customer THROUGH THEIR AREA and only that way
(customers.cus_area_id -> area_master.arm_city_id -> city_master.ctm_id);
customers.cus_city is free text and must never be matched on. A customer
with no area is in no city.

prpMatchPriority makes "a rule naming one CUSTOMER beats one naming their
CITY" data rather than an assumption: CUSTOMER 4, AREA 3, CITY 2,
CUSTOMER_GROUP 1 — narrowest first. EXCLUDE beats INCLUDE at equal priority.

Read only when prm_cust_scope = \'LIST\'.

── WRITING THIS MODEL ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
NEVER set custId / custGroupId / areaId / cityId, and never use the four
nested `connect`s. Postgres computes those columns and rejects any write.
Set prpKind + prpScopeId; read the relations back.

── MIGRATION ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
prisma migrate cannot emit a generated column. The four must be
hand-authored, e.g.
prp_cust_id uuid GENERATED ALWAYS AS
(CASE WHEN prp_kind::text = \'CUSTOMER\' THEN prp_scope_id END) STORED
The `@default(dbgenerated())` below is the no-argument form: it stops every
later `migrate dev` emitting an ALTER COLUMN ... DROP DEFAULT that Postgres
rejects with 42601 on a generated column.

The FKs are ON UPDATE NO ACTION, not CASCADE — Postgres forbids a cascading
update on a key containing a generated column. Costs nothing: every master
key here is a uuidv7() that is never reissued.

CHECK ck_prp_kind, ck_prp_slno and ck_prp_match_priority are enforced in the
service, not the DB.

DB-only objects to hand-author:
ux_prp_row       UNIQUE (prm_id, kind, scope_id) WHERE NOT deleted
ix_prp_scheme    (prm_id, match_priority DESC) INCLUDE (...), partial
ix_prp_cust / ix_prp_cust_group / ix_prp_area / ix_prp_city, all partial'
}

Table promotion_scheme_slab {
  prsId String [pk]
  prsPrmId String [not null]
  prsSlno Int [not null, default: 1]
  prsBenefit String [not null]
  prsExceeds Decimal [not null, default: 0]
  prsUpto Decimal
  prsEach Decimal [not null, default: 1]
  prsIsRepeat Boolean [not null, default: false]
  prsMaxRepeats Int [not null, default: 0]
  prsFreeItemId String
  prsFreeUnitId String
  prsFreeQty Decimal [not null, default: 0]
  prsFreeStockCheck Boolean [not null, default: true]
  prsDiscPerc Decimal [not null, default: 0]
  prsDiscQty Decimal [not null, default: 0]
  prsDiscAmt Decimal [not null, default: 0]
  prsFixedPrice Decimal
  prsMaxBenefitAmt Decimal [not null, default: 0]
  prsNotes String
  prsIsActive Boolean [not null, default: true]
  prsIsDeleted Boolean [not null, default: false]
  prsSyncDate DateTime
  prsCreatedOn DateTime [default: `now()`, not null]
  prsCreatedBy String
  prsModifiedOn DateTime
  prsModifiedBy String
  scheme promotion_scheme [not null]
  freeItem item_master
  freeUnit item_unit_conversion

  Note: 'sales.promotion_scheme_slab — §5, the offer bands.

One row = one band. Read as: when the measured value is between prsExceeds
and prsUpto, give the benefit; and if prsIsRepeat, give it once per prsEach
above prsExceeds instead of once in total.

"the measured value" is RUPEES when prm_apply_on is BILL_AMOUNT or
ITEM_AMOUNT, and QUANTITY when it is BILL_QTY or ITEM_QTY — one column pair
serving both, exactly as the loyalty slabs do.

Bands are ROWS, not columns: 0-999 -> 2%, 1000-4999 -> 5%, 5000+ -> 8% is
three rows.

── The benefit is ENFORCED, not merely documented ──────────────────────
prsBenefit is a copy of the header\'s prmBenefit tied to it by a COMPOSITE
foreign key (prs_prm_id, prs_benefit) -> (prm_id, prm_benefit), which is
why PromotionScheme carries the otherwise-redundant
@@unique([prmId, prmBenefit]). A percentage-discount scheme therefore
CANNOT physically hold a free-item band. This composite FK also carries the
ON DELETE CASCADE, so no second plain FK on prsPrmId is declared.

The other half of that pair — ck_prs_benefit_columns, "the benefit column
is filled and the others are empty" — is a CHECK, so it lives in the
service instead of the migration, along with ck_prs_band, ck_prs_each,
ck_prs_amounts, ck_prs_free_unit_pair and ck_prs_slno. The FK still stops
the header and its bands disagreeing; the service is what stops a
DISC_PERC band being saved with a fixed price in it.

DB-only objects to hand-author:
ux_prs_band      UNIQUE (prm_id, exceeds, COALESCE(free_item_id, nil uuid))
WHERE NOT deleted — so "spend 5000, get a mug AND a pen"
is two rows at one threshold, while two rows claiming the
same band with nothing to separate them stays impossible
ix_prs_scheme    (prm_id, benefit, exceeds) INCLUDE (...), partial
ix_prs_free_item / ix_prs_free_unit, partial'
}

Table promotion_usage {
  pruId String [not null]
  pruAccYear String [not null, note: 'FY of the EVENT, and the partition key.']
  pruCompId String [not null]
  pruBranchId String
  pruPrmId String [not null]
  pruCustId String [note: 'NULL = walk-in / cash sale, which is also why the per-customer cap index
is partial on this column: an anonymous sale has no cap to check.']
  pruSchemeCode String
  pruSchemeName String
  pruBenefit String [note: 'Snapshot of promotion_scheme.prmBenefit: FREE_ITEM | DISC_PERC |
DISC_AMT | DISC_PER_ITEM | FIXED_PRICE. ck_pru_benefit must cover every
value that column can hold — prmBenefit itself carries no CHECK, so a
new value added there needs one added here in the same migration.']
  pruSrcModule String [not null, default: 'SALES']
  pruSrcDocType String [not null, default: 'SALE_BILL']
  pruSrcDocId String [not null]
  pruSrcAccYear String [note: 'The SOURCE\'s FY, not this row\'s.']
  pruSrcDocRefno String [note: 'Snapshot, so a campaign report prints the bill number without joining a
partitioned table across every year the campaign ran.']
  pruDocDate DateTime [not null]
  pruBaseAmount Decimal [not null, default: 0, note: 'Value the benefit came off.']
  pruBaseQty Decimal [not null, default: 0]
  pruBenefitAmt Decimal [not null, default: 0, note: 'Rupees given away. POSITIVE on a normal row, NEGATIVE on a reversal
(ck_pru_sign).']
  pruFreeQty Decimal [not null, default: 0, note: 'Units given away, same sign rule.']
  pruLineCount Int [not null, default: 0, note: 'Lines the scheme touched.']
  pruCouponId String [note: 'The coupon presented, when the campaign was coupon-gated.']
  pruIsReversal Boolean [not null, default: false]
  pruReversalOfId String
  pruReversalOfAccYear String
  pruReversalReason String
  pruUserId String
  pruDeviceId String
  pruRemarks String
  pruIsActive Boolean [not null, default: true]
  pruIsDeleted Boolean [not null, default: false]
  pruSyncDate DateTime
  pruCreatedOn DateTime [default: `now()`, not null]
  pruCreatedBy String
  pruModifiedOn DateTime
  pruModifiedBy String
  company companys [not null]
  branch branch_master
  scheme promotion_scheme [not null, note: 'Restrict, not Cascade: a campaign that has already given something away
must not be deletable. Close it instead.']
  customer customers
  coupon loyalty_coupon
  device device_master
  user user_master
  reversalOf promotion_usage
  reversedBy promotion_usage [not null]

  indexes {
    (pruId, pruAccYear) [pk]
  }

  Note: 'What a promotion campaign actually GAVE AWAY — one row per (document,
scheme, direction).

The PromotionScheme masters say what a campaign PROMISES; nothing until now
recorded what it DELIVERED. Without that, two questions have no answer, and
both are asked at the till in the moment, before the benefit is applied:

the CAP     "has this customer already used this campaign twice?"
-> ix_pru_cust_scheme
the BUDGET  "what has this campaign given away in total, everywhere?"
-> ix_pru_scheme_total

LIST-partitioned by pruAccYear, the year of the EVENT, like LoyaltyLedger
and LoyaltyCouponTxn — hence the composite PK. Partitions
(sales.promotion_usage_2026_2027) come from
public.ensure_acc_year_partitions(\'YYYY-YYYY\'), which
20260921180000_add_promotion_usage taught about this table.

Scheme identity is SNAPSHOTTED (pruSchemeCode / _Name / _Benefit) so a
campaign report does not change meaning when the scheme is renamed or
retyped next season.

A reversal is a row of the same shape with NEGATIVE amounts and
pruIsReversal = true, naming the row it undoes — the AccBillAdjustment and
LoyaltyLedger discipline, and what makes every running total a plain SUM
with no CASE in it.'
}

