Table stock_ageing_slab {
  sagId String [pk]
  sagCompanyId String [note: 'NULL = the default ladder, used whole by any company with no ladder of
its own. Never merged with a company\'s own slabs.']
  sagSlabNo Int [not null, note: '1-based position in the ladder: display order and stable identity.']
  sagLabel String [not null, note: 'What the report column says — "31-60 days". Free text on purpose: it is
never parsed back into the bounds below.']
  sagFromDays Int [not null, note: 'Inclusive lower bound, in days.']
  sagToDays Int [note: 'Inclusive upper bound, in days. NULL = open ended; exactly one slab per
ladder may be NULL, and it is the last one.']
  sagIsActive Boolean [not null, default: true]
  sagIsDeleted Boolean [not null, default: false]
  sagSyncDate DateTime
  sagCreatedOn DateTime [default: `now()`, not null]
  sagCreatedBy String
  sagModifiedOn DateTime
  sagModifiedBy String
  company companys

  Note: 'stock.stock_ageing_slab — the age buckets an ageing report sums into:
"0-30 / 31-60 / 61-90 / 91-180 / over 180". One row is one bucket; the rows
sharing a sagCompanyId are one LADDER.

sagCompanyId NULL = the DEFAULT ladder, used by any company that has not
authored one of its own. Unlike the tracking presets — where a company row
merges over a shared row code by code — this fallback is ALL OR NOTHING:
fn_sag_effective returns a company\'s own slabs if it has any, otherwise the
default ones, never a mixture. Merging two ladders would put one holding in
two buckets and the report would stop summing to the balance. A company that
wants a dairy\'s 0-3-7 inserts its FULL ladder, not a patch over the default.

The bucket a holding falls in is decided by the age in DAYS measured from
whatever stpAgeingBasis the resolved StockTrackPolicy names (inward date,
last-in date, or manufacture date) — this table only says where the cuts
are, never how age is computed.

The range is HALF-OPEN in the database: [sagFromDays, sagToDays + 1). Read
the columns as inclusive-inclusive — 31-60 means both 31 and 60 — and let
the exclusion constraint do the arithmetic. It is written that way rather
than as an inclusive \'[]\' range because an inclusive upper bound of int4\'s
maximum overflows when PostgreSQL normalises it to upper + 1.

sagToDays NULL = OPEN ENDED, "and everything older". There must be exactly
one such slab per ladder, and it must be the last one: without it the oldest
stock falls out of every bucket and the report silently under-reports.

WRITE RULES:
* a ladder is contiguous and gapless. The overlap constraint stops two
slabs claiming the same day; nothing at the database level stops a GAP
(31-60 followed by 71-90), so the screen must.
* REORDERING A LADDER NEEDS A DEFERRED TRANSACTION. Shrinking 0-30 to
0-14 collides with 31-60 until that row moves too, so a row-at-a-time
save cannot reorder a ladder at all. Issue
SET CONSTRAINTS stock.ex_sag_overlap DEFERRED
first, write every row, then COMMIT. The constraint is DEFERRABLE
INITIALLY IMMEDIATE, so callers that do not ask for this are unaffected.
* sagSlabNo is the display order and the stable identity within a ladder,
1-based. (sagCompanyId, sagSlabNo) is unique among undeleted rows.

DB-only objects Prisma cannot express — hand-authored in the migration and
deliberately NOT declared here (both carry a WHERE, and Prisma ignores
indexes with predicates):
ex_sag_overlap  EXCLUDE USING gist over (company, int4range(from, to+1))
WHERE active AND NOT deleted. Needs btree_gist. The
company is COALESCEd to the nil uuid because EXCLUDE never
conflicts on NULL — without it two default slabs could
claim the same day. A default slab and a company slab
covering the same days DO coexist, and must: they belong
to different ladders.
ux_sag_slab_no  unique on (COALESCE(company, nil uuid), slab_no) WHERE NOT
deleted — COALESCEd for the same reason, a plain unique
index treats every NULL as distinct and would let the seed
insert two slab 1s into the default ladder.
plus ck_sag_days (from >= 0, and to >= from when to is given) and
ck_sag_slab_no (>= 1).

sagCreatedBy carries no foreign key in the migration, unlike
[[StockTrackPolicy]] and [[StockTrackPreset]], so no relation is declared —
adding one here without adding fk_sag_created_by to the DDL would show up as
schema drift on the next migrate.'
}

Table stock_balance {
  sblId String [pk]
  sblCompanyId String [not null]
  sblBranchId String [not null]
  sblTenantId String
  sblGodownId String [not null, note: 'The shelf — an inventory.godown_locations(gdl_id). Branch and godown live
HERE and not on the lot: the same batch held in four godowns is four
balance rows and one lot.']
  sblItemId String [not null]
  sblLotId String [not null]
  sblBaseUomId String [not null, note: 'The item\'s BASE unit — an item_unit_conversion(iuc_id), NEVER an
item_unit_master(unit_id). Every quantity on this row is in this unit.']
  sblBucket String [not null, default: 'SALEABLE']
  sblInQty Decimal [not null, default: 0]
  sblOutQty Decimal [not null, default: 0]
  sblFreeInQty Decimal [not null, default: 0]
  sblFreeOutQty Decimal [not null, default: 0]
  sblOnHandQty Decimal [note: 'GENERATED ALWAYS ... STORED as in + freeIn - out - freeOut. What is
PHYSICALLY here, free goods included. Read-only, never sent.']
  sblReservedQty Decimal [not null, default: 0, note: 'Here, but promised to an order. Owned by stock_reservation.']
  sblTransitInQty Decimal [not null, default: 0, note: 'Sent from another branch, not yet received here. Owned by stock_transit,
and NOT part of on hand: it is not here yet.']
  sblAvailableQty Decimal [note: 'GENERATED ALWAYS ... STORED as onHand - reserved. What may still be SOLD,
and the figure a billing screen checks. Read-only, never sent.']
  sblAvgCostRate Decimal [not null, default: 0]
  sblAvgCostRateWot Decimal [not null, default: 0]
  sblStockValue Decimal [not null, default: 0]
  sblStockValueWot Decimal [not null, default: 0]
  sblFirstInDate DateTime
  sblLastInDate DateTime
  sblLastOutDate DateTime
  sblBatchNo String
  sblMrp Decimal
  sblSalePrice Decimal
  sblExpiryDate DateTime
  sblSupplierId String
  sblRowVersion BigInt [not null, default: 1]
  sblIsActive Boolean [not null, default: true]
  sblIsDeleted Boolean [not null, default: false]
  sblSyncDate DateTime
  sblCreatedOn DateTime [default: `now()`, not null]
  sblCreatedBy String
  sblModifiedOn DateTime
  sblModifiedBy String
  company companys [not null]
  branch branch_master [not null]
  godown godown_locations [not null]
  item item_master [not null]
  lot stock_lot [not null]
  baseUom item_unit_conversion [not null]
  supplier suppliers

  Note: 'stock.stock_balance — the CURRENT position. One row per holding.

[[StockLedger]] is the truth and this is the answer: every figure here is
derivable by replaying the ledger, and is kept here only so the billing
screen does not have to. The grain is
(company, branch, godown, item, lot, bucket) — ux_sbl_scope — which is one
row per "this batch of this item, on this shelf, in this condition".

NOT PARTITIONED, and deliberately without an acc-year column. A balance is
as-of-now, not as-of-a-year: carrying stock into a new fiscal year must not
mean copying every holding into a new partition, and "what is on the shelf"
has exactly one answer regardless of which year the movements that produced
it were posted in. Year-wise questions are ledger questions.

ENGINE-MAINTAINED. The posting engine owns this table — on this deployment
stock-voucher-posting.helper.ts, doing what the share\'s fn_sml_apply() does:
it upserts on ux_sbl_scope as ledger rows land, and it alone writes the four
accumulators and the identity cache. Application code that writes sblInQty / sblOutQty /
sblFreeInQty / sblFreeOutQty is writing a number the next posting will
overwrite, and has moved stock without a ledger row to explain it. Post to
StockLedger instead — always.

THE FOUR ACCUMULATORS, NOT A SINGLE BALANCE. In and out are kept apart
because a concurrent inward and outward then touch different columns of the
same row rather than fighting over one, and because "how much moved" is a
question the balance can answer without opening the ledger. Free in and
free out are kept apart again so margin reporting can separate what was
paid for from what was not — but free goods ARE stock: they sit on the
shelf, they get sold, and sblOnHandQty counts them.

THREE QUANTITIES, THREE MEANINGS, and confusing them is the classic stock
bug:
sblOnHandQty     what is physically here      (in + freeIn - out - freeOut)
sblReservedQty   here, but promised to an order   — owned by stock_reservation
sblAvailableQty  what may still be sold       (onHand - reserved)
sblTransitInQty  sent from another branch, NOT here yet — owned by
stock_transit, and NOT part of on hand
A billing screen checks AVAILABLE, a physical count reconciles against ON
HAND, and a transfer receipt clears TRANSIT. sblAvailableQty repeats the
on-hand arithmetic instead of referencing it because PostgreSQL forbids a
generated column reading another generated column.

THE IDENTITY CACHE — sblBatchNo, sblMrp, sblSalePrice, sblExpiryDate,
sblSupplierId — is COPIED from [[StockLot]] by the same trigger, and the lot
remains the owner. It is duplicated so that near-expiry, MRP-wise and
supplier-wise stock reports are covering-index reads on this table alone;
when the two ever disagree, the lot is right.

AGEING ANCHORS. sblFirstInDate / sblLastInDate answer "how long has this sat
on THIS shelf"; the lot\'s sltFirstInwardDate answers "how old is this stock
in the chain", which a transfer must not reset. StockTrackPolicy.
stpAgeingBasis picks which one an ageing report measures from, and
[[StockAgeingSlab]] says where the cuts are.

NEGATIVE STOCK IS REPRESENTABLE ON PURPOSE. ck_sbl_accum keeps the four
accumulators non-negative, but nothing stops on-hand going below zero —
a StockTrackPolicy of ALLOW is a real business choice (an offline till that
must keep billing, a receipt keyed after the sale it supplied). The
exception report reads ix_sbl_negative.

WRITE RULES:
* NEVER write sblOnHandQty or sblAvailableQty — GENERATED ALWAYS ...
STORED, and Postgres rejects any write to them.
* NEVER write the accumulators or the identity cache from application
code; post a ledger row.
* sblReservedQty and sblTransitInQty belong to stock_reservation and
stock_transit respectively, and neither may go negative.

Vocabulary, enforced by a CHECK constraint in the migration:
sblBucket  SALEABLE | DAMAGED | QUARANTINE | EXPIRED | SAMPLE — the same
five as StockLedger.smlBucket, and one balance row each,
because saleable stock and damaged stock must never be added
together by accident

DB-only objects Prisma cannot express — hand-authored in the migration and
deliberately NOT declared here. EVERY index on this table carries a WHERE,
and Prisma ignores indexes with predicates:
ux_sbl_scope       ONE ROW PER HOLDING, and the upsert target of
fn_sml_apply(). Partial on NOT deleted.
ix_sbl_lookup      the billing screen\'s stock lookup — what
/master-lookups/item-price asks, answered without
touching the heap
ix_sbl_item_chain  item-wise stock across a branch\'s godowns, and the
chain-wide "who else has this?" behind an inter-store
transfer suggestion
ix_sbl_ageing      ageing, on shelf age
ix_sbl_supplier    supplier-wise stock, straight off the cached supplier
ix_sbl_expiry      near expiry and expired-stock blocking
ix_sbl_mrp         MRP-wise and selling-price-wise summaries
ix_sbl_negative    the negative-stock exception report'
}

Table stock_demand_daily {
  sddId String [pk]
  sddCompanyId String [not null]
  sddBranchId String [not null]
  sddTenantId String
  sddItemId String [not null]
  sddBaseUomId String [not null]
  sddDemandDate DateTime [not null]
  sddDayOfWeek Int [not null, default: 0, note: 'Denormalised for seasonality models. 0 = Sunday, matching EXTRACT(dow).']
  sddSoldQty Decimal [not null, default: 0]
  sddFreeQty Decimal [not null, default: 0]
  sddReturnQty Decimal [not null, default: 0]
  sddNetQty Decimal [note: 'GENERATED ALWAYS ... STORED as sold + free - returned. Read-only.']
  sddPromoQty Decimal [not null, default: 0, note: 'Sold while a promotion was running: real sales, but not the baseline.
A SUBSET of sddSoldQty, not an addition to it.']
  sddBillCount Int [not null, default: 0]
  sddLineCount Int [not null, default: 0]
  sddCustomerCount Int [not null, default: 0]
  sddRevenueWot Decimal [not null, default: 0]
  sddCogsWot Decimal [not null, default: 0]
  sddMarginWot Decimal [note: 'GENERATED ALWAYS ... STORED as revenue - COGS. Read-only.']
  sddStockoutFlag Boolean [not null, default: false, note: 'On hand hit zero at some point that day: demand was CENSORED, and the
figures above are a floor, not a measurement.']
  sddStockoutHours Decimal [not null, default: 0]
  sddClosedFlag Boolean [not null, default: false, note: 'The branch was SHUT. Exclude from a moving average — never read as zero
demand.']
  sddOpenQtyStart Decimal [not null, default: 0]
  sddCloseQtyEnd Decimal [not null, default: 0]
  sddRebuiltOn DateTime [default: `now()`, not null, note: 'When this row was last recomputed. A stale date on a recent day means the
rebuild job did not run — otherwise invisible.']
  sddIsDeleted Boolean [not null, default: false]
  sddSyncDate DateTime
  sddCreatedOn DateTime [default: `now()`, not null]
  company companys [not null]
  branch branch_master [not null]
  item item_master [not null]
  baseUom item_unit_conversion [not null]

  Note: 'stock.stock_demand_daily — what actually sold, per branch, per day.

The input every forecast needs and no transactional table can serve.
Aggregating a year of SaleBillItem across fifty branches on every run of a
replenishment job is not a plan. This is the pre-aggregate, rebuilt by
fn_sdd_rebuild() from [[StockLedger]] — which means it can ALWAYS be
recomputed and never has to be trusted blindly.

sddStockoutFlag IS THE COLUMN THAT MAKES FORECASTS HONEST. If an item was
out of stock on Tuesday it sold zero on Tuesday, and a model that reads
that as "demand was zero" will under-forecast the fastest movers for ever
and keep them out of stock — the failure feeds itself. Censored demand has
to be MARKED at the point where it is still knowable; afterwards a zero is
indistinguishable from a zero. sddStockoutHours says how much of the day
was lost, so a partial day is not thrown away whole.

sddPromoQty exists for the same reason in the other direction: a week of
buy-one-get-one is not the baseline, and a model that treats it as one will
over-order for the month after. It is a subset of what sold, not an
addition to it.

sddClosedFlag is a THIRD kind of zero. Shut is not "nobody wanted it": such
a day must be EXCLUDED from a moving average, not averaged in as demand.
Three different zeros, three different columns, because a forecast that
cannot tell them apart is worse than no forecast.

sddDayOfWeek is denormalised so a weekday/seasonality model does not
compute it a million times. 0 = Sunday, matching EXTRACT(dow).

NOT PARTITIONED. The read is a WINDOW of days for one item — "the last
ninety days" crosses a year boundary four times out of five — and a
partition per year would split every one of those.

WRITE RULES:
* NEVER write sddNetQty or sddMarginWot — both are GENERATED ALWAYS ...
STORED.
* This table is DERIVED. Do not patch a row to make a report look right;
fix the ledger and re-run fn_sdd_rebuild(), or the next rebuild silently
discards the correction.
* sddRebuiltOn says when the row was last recomputed — a stale date on a
recent day means the job did not run, which is otherwise invisible.

DB-only objects Prisma cannot express — hand-authored in the migration and
deliberately NOT declared here (every one carries a WHERE):
ux_sdd_scope   one row per (company, branch, item, date)
ix_sdd_series  the forecasting read: one item, one branch, a window of
days, in order
ix_sdd_chain   chain-wide demand for one item across every branch — the
head-office allocation question'
}

Table stock_forecast {
  sfcId String [pk]
  sfcCompanyId String [not null]
  sfcBranchId String [not null]
  sfcTenantId String
  sfcItemId String [not null]
  sfcBaseUomId String [not null]
  sfcForecastDate DateTime [not null, note: 'The day forecast FOR — not the day computed, which is sfcComputedOn.']
  sfcHorizonDays Int [not null, default: 30]
  sfcMethod String [not null, default: 'MOVING_AVG', note: 'In the unique key: two methods may run side by side on the same history.']
  sfcForecastQty Decimal [not null, default: 0]
  sfcForecastLower Decimal
  sfcForecastUpper Decimal
  sfcConfidencePerc Decimal
  sfcDailyRate Decimal [not null, default: 0]
  sfcHistoryDays Int [not null, default: 0]
  sfcStockoutDays Int [not null, default: 0, note: 'Censored days WITHIN that history — cannot exceed sfcHistoryDays.']
  sfcTrendSlope Decimal
  sfcSeasonalityIndex Decimal
  sfcModelParams Json [note: 'Alpha/beta/gamma, window lengths, whatever the method needs. jsonb so a
new method costs no column and no migration.']
  sfcMape Decimal
  sfcRmse Decimal
  sfcBias Decimal [note: 'Separate from error on purpose: consistently over-ordering and randomly
missing are different diseases with different cures.']
  sfcBacktestDays Int
  sfcOnHandQty Decimal [not null, default: 0]
  sfcPipelineQty Decimal [not null, default: 0, note: 'Ordered or in transit: stock already coming, which MUST be netted off
before suggesting more. Forgetting this is how a chain double-orders.']
  sfcSafetyStock Decimal [not null, default: 0]
  sfcReorderPoint Decimal [not null, default: 0]
  sfcSuggestedOrderQty Decimal [not null, default: 0]
  sfcDaysOfCover Decimal
  sfcExpectedStockoutOn DateTime
  sfcPreferredSupplierId String
  sfcAcceptedQty Decimal [note: 'Set when a buyer OVERRIDES the suggestion, so the next back-test knows
the order it sees was not the one the model asked for.']
  sfcAcceptedBy String
  sfcAcceptedOn DateTime
  sfcComputedOn DateTime [default: `now()`, not null]
  sfcComputedBy String [note: 'Which job, service or notebook produced this row.']
  sfcIsDeleted Boolean [not null, default: false]
  sfcSyncDate DateTime
  sfcCreatedOn DateTime [default: `now()`, not null]
  company companys [not null]
  branch branch_master [not null]
  item item_master [not null]
  baseUom item_unit_conversion [not null]
  preferredSupplier suppliers

  Note: 'stock.stock_forecast — what we expect to sell, and what to order.

THE MATHS IS NOT IN THE DATABASE, and that is the design. A forecasting
model written in PL/pgSQL cannot be changed without a migration, cannot be
back-tested against last year without running last year, and cannot be
swapped when the retailer discovers Croston fits their slow movers better
than exponential smoothing. It WILL be changed.

So the schema holds the three things a model needs to be useful and does
not hold the model: the INPUTS ([[StockDemandDaily]]), the SETTINGS
([[StockReorderPolicy]]) and the OUTPUTS (this table). A nightly job, the
API layer, or somebody\'s notebook writes rows here; every screen reads them
the same way regardless of what produced them, and sfcComputedBy says which
it was.

THE ERROR COLUMNS ARE NOT OPTIONAL. A forecast without a measured error is
a number somebody made up. sfcMape, sfcRmse and sfcBias are written by the
same run that writes the forecast, computed against the period just past,
so a buyer can see that this item\'s model has been wrong by 40% for three
months and stop trusting it. BIAS IS SEPARATE FROM ERROR on purpose:
consistently over-ordering and randomly missing are different diseases with
different cures, and a single accuracy number hides both.

ONE ROW PER (item, branch, forecast date, horizon, METHOD). Keeping the
method in the key means two models run side by side against the same
history and be compared on it — which is the only honest way to choose one.

sfcPipelineQty IS THE COLUMN A REPLENISHMENT BUG LIVES IN. Stock already
ordered or in transit must be netted off before suggesting more; forgetting
it is how a chain double-orders, twice a week, until somebody notices the
warehouse is full.

sfcAcceptedQty is set when a BUYER OVERRIDES the suggestion, so the next
back-test knows the order it sees in the purchase history was not the one
the model asked for. Without it every override silently becomes evidence
against the model.

sfcModelParams is jsonb — alpha/beta/gamma, window lengths, whatever the
method needs — so a new method costs no column and no migration.

sfcForecastDate IS THE DAY FORECAST FOR, not the day computed. The day
computed is sfcComputedOn. Confusing the two makes every back-test wrong.

WRITE RULES:
* sfcStockoutDays cannot exceed sfcHistoryDays (ck_sfc_stockout_days) —
it is a count of censored days WITHIN the history the model saw.
* sfcAcceptedQty and sfcAcceptedOn move together (ck_sfc_accepted).
* The confidence band is optional, but if both ends are given the upper
must not be below the lower (ck_sfc_band).

Vocabulary, enforced by a CHECK constraint in the migration:
sfcMethod  MOVING_AVG | WMA | SES | HOLT | HOLT_WINTERS | CROSTON |
REGRESSION | MANUAL | EXTERNAL
plus ck_sfc_horizon, ck_sfc_confidence and ck_sfc_qty.

DB-only objects Prisma cannot express — hand-authored in the migration and
deliberately NOT declared here (every one carries a WHERE):
ux_sfc_scope      one row per item/branch/date/horizon/method
ix_sfc_action     the buying screen: this branch, latest run, worst first
ix_sfc_item_chain chain view for one item — which branch runs out first
ix_sfc_accuracy   model comparison: how has each method actually done'
}

Table stock_item_cost {
  sicId String [pk]
  sicCompanyId String [not null]
  sicBranchId String [not null]
  sicItemId String [not null]
  sicBaseUomId String [not null, note: 'The item\'s BASE unit — an item_unit_conversion(iuc_id), NEVER an
item_unit_master(unit_id). sicTotalQty is in this unit, so the average is
a rate per base unit and never per "case".']
  sicTotalQty Decimal [not null, default: 0]
  sicTotalValue Decimal [not null, default: 0]
  sicTotalValueWot Decimal [not null, default: 0]
  sicAvgCostRate Decimal [not null, default: 0, note: 'Stored, not derived — the rate must survive sicTotalQty reaching zero.']
  sicAvgCostRateWot Decimal [not null, default: 0]
  sicLastPurchaseRate Decimal [not null, default: 0]
  sicLastPurchaseDate DateTime
  sicLastSaleRate Decimal [not null, default: 0]
  sicLastSaleDate DateTime
  sicMaxCostRate Decimal [not null, default: 0, note: 'The highest rate this item has ever been received at — a
replacement-cost valuation asks for it, and it cannot be recovered once
lots close.']
  sicRowVersion BigInt [not null, default: 1]
  sicIsDeleted Boolean [not null, default: false]
  sicSyncDate DateTime
  sicCreatedOn DateTime [default: `now()`, not null]
  sicCreatedBy String
  sicModifiedOn DateTime
  sicModifiedBy String
  company companys [not null]
  branch branch_master [not null]
  item item_master [not null]
  baseUom item_unit_conversion [not null]

  Note: 'stock.stock_item_cost — the MOVING WEIGHTED AVERAGE, one row per
(company, branch, item). The other half of the valuation answer.

[[StockLot]] carries each lot\'s OWN cost, which is what makes FIFO, LIFO and
LOT_ACTUAL valuation a read rather than a replay. This table carries the
running average across all of an item\'s lots in a branch. BOTH are
maintained on every posting, always — so StockTrackPolicy.stpValuationMethod
SELECTS an answer instead of triggering a rebuild, and switching a company
from average to FIFO costs nothing.

BRANCH-LEVEL, NOT GODOWN-LEVEL. Moving a case from the back store to the
shop floor must not change what the stock cost; a per-godown average would
let an internal transfer move value. Godown-wise value is stock_balance\'s
sblStockValue, which is a distribution of this, not a separate truth. It is
also not company-wide: two branches buying the same item at different rates
have different costs, and averaging them would let one branch\'s bad buying
inflate the other\'s margin.

NOT LOT-LEVEL EITHER, on purpose — that is exactly what the lot\'s own
sltCostRate is. This row is what the average costs when the lot identity is
deliberately ignored.

ENGINE-MAINTAINED. The posting engine owns it — on this deployment that is
stock-voucher-posting.helper.ts (phase 5), doing what the share\'s
fn_sml_apply() does: an inward adds qty and value and recomputes the
average, an outward removes qty at the CURRENT average and leaves the rate
alone — which is what "moving average" means, and why an outward can never
change the cost of what remains. Application code that writes these columns
is writing a number the next posting will overwrite. Post to [[StockLedger]]
instead.

THE AVERAGE IS STORED, NOT DERIVED. sicAvgCostRate is a plain column rather
than value/qty as a generated one, because qty legitimately reaches zero
when the last unit is sold and the rate must SURVIVE that: the next inward
of an item that went to nil is not a fresh start, and a division by zero
would make it one.

sicMaxCostRate is the highest rate this item has ever been received at. It
is kept because a replacement-cost valuation asks for it and because it
cannot be recovered afterwards — closed lots are still there, but the
question is asked often enough that scanning them every time is not
reasonable.

WOT columns are WITHOUT TAX. Where GST is claimable the value that belongs
in the accounts is the ex-tax one, so both are carried and the caller picks
per the company\'s tax treatment rather than re-deriving one from the other.

WRITE RULES:
* NEVER write sicTotalQty / sicTotalValue / sicTotalValueWot /
sicAvgCostRate / sicAvgCostRateWot / sicMaxCostRate, nor the last
purchase and last sale columns — all are engine-maintained.
* There is no sicIsActive: a cost row is not something a user switches
off. sicIsDeleted exists only for the partial index predicate.

DB-only objects Prisma cannot express — hand-authored in the migration and
deliberately NOT declared here (it carries a WHERE, and Prisma ignores
indexes with predicates):
ux_sic_scope  ONE ROW PER (company, branch, item), and the upsert target
of fn_sml_apply(). Partial on NOT deleted.'
}

Table stock_ledger {
  smlId String [not null]
  smlCompanyId String [not null]
  smlBranchId String [not null]
  smlTenantId String
  smlAccYear String [not null, note: 'The PARTITION KEY, and half the primary key. See PARTITIONED BY above.']
  smlGodownId String [not null, note: 'Where the stock physically is — an inventory.godown_locations(gdl_id),
the self-referencing WAREHOUSE/ZONE/AISLE/RACK/SHELF/BIN tree. Never
nullable: an unlocated movement cannot be reversed, counted or
transferred. BranchMaster.brDefaultGodownId resolves it for screens with
no godown selector.']
  smlItemId String [not null]
  smlLotId String [not null]
  smlUomId String [not null, note: 'The unit the document was keyed in, and the item\'s base unit. BOTH are
item_unit_conversion(iuc_id), NEVER item_unit_master(unit_id) — the stock
a bill consumes must be keyed the same way the bill keys it
(SaleBillItem.sbiItemUnitId). Both stored so a report can show "3 cases"
and still sum pieces.']
  smlBaseUomId String [not null]
  smlToBaseFactor Decimal [not null, default: 1]
  smlSrcModule String [not null, note: 'The (module, docType, docId) triple used by acc_tender_detail,
txn_status_log, txn_hold and acc_voucher_doc_register. A new document type
that moves stock costs one CHECK value and nothing else.']
  smlSrcDocType String [not null]
  smlSrcDocId String [not null]
  smlSrcAccYear String [note: 'The DOCUMENT\'s own year, which is not always smlAccYear.']
  smlSrcRefno String
  smlLineNo Int [not null, default: 1]
  smlSplitNo Int [not null, default: 1]
  smlTxnType String [not null]
  smlDirection Int [not null, note: '+1 in, -1 out. See the vocabulary note above for why this is redundant
against smlTxnType on purpose.']
  smlBucket String [not null, default: 'SALEABLE']
  smlDocDate DateTime [not null]
  smlDocDatetime DateTime [default: `now()`, not null]
  smlPostedOn DateTime [default: `now()`, not null]
  smlQty Decimal [not null, default: 0]
  smlBaseQty Decimal [not null, default: 0]
  smlFreeQty Decimal [not null, default: 0]
  smlFreeBaseQty Decimal [not null, default: 0]
  smlWeightQty Decimal [not null, default: 0]
  smlSignedBaseQty Decimal [note: 'GENERATED ALWAYS ... STORED as direction * (baseQty + freeBaseQty). The
one column stock_balance reads and every quantity report sums. Read-only,
never sent.']
  smlCostRate Decimal [not null, default: 0]
  smlCostValue Decimal [not null, default: 0]
  smlCostRateWot Decimal [not null, default: 0]
  smlCostValueWot Decimal [not null, default: 0]
  smlLandedRate Decimal [not null, default: 0]
  smlLandedValue Decimal [not null, default: 0]
  smlDocRate Decimal [not null, default: 0, note: 'What the DOCUMENT said, which is the selling price on a sale. Kept beside
cost so margin needs no join back to SaleBillItem.']
  smlDocRateWot Decimal [not null, default: 0]
  smlDocAmountWot Decimal [not null, default: 0]
  smlMrp Decimal [note: 'Snapshots from the lot, so batch/MRP-wise reports read one table.']
  smlBatchNo String
  smlExpiryDate DateTime
  smlIsReversal Boolean [not null, default: false, note: 'smlReversesId points at the smlId being reversed. It carries NO foreign
key — the target lives in another partition as often as not, and a
composite FK back into a partitioned parent would buy nothing that
ux_sml_reversal does not already guarantee. Both columns move together
per ck_sml_reversal.']
  smlReversesId String
  smlReasonId String
  smlPartyId String [note: 'Customer or supplier, per smlSrcModule. Polymorphic, so no foreign key.']
  smlNarration String
  smlIsDeleted Boolean [not null, default: false]
  smlSyncDate DateTime
  smlCreatedOn DateTime [default: `now()`, not null]
  smlCreatedBy String
  company companys [not null]
  branch branch_master [not null]
  godown godown_locations [not null]
  item item_master [not null]
  lot stock_lot [not null]
  uom item_unit_conversion [not null]
  baseUom item_unit_conversion [not null]
  reason stock_reason_master

  indexes {
    (smlId, smlAccYear) [pk]
  }

  Note: 'stock.stock_ledger — every movement. The truth.

APPEND-ONLY. Never updated in place, never deleted. A cancelled bill does
not remove its ledger rows, it writes REVERSING ones — because a stock
figure that can change retrospectively is a stock figure nobody can
reconcile, and because in an offline chain the cancellation may arrive
after the day was closed. A BEFORE DELETE trigger raises rather than
trusting convention, so Prisma\'s delete/deleteMany WILL fail here by
design; smlIsDeleted exists for the soft-delete predicate the partial
indexes are built on, not as an escape hatch.

GRAIN — one row per (source document, line, split, godown, lot, direction).
That is exactly the grain SaleBillItem already emits (sbiLineNo ×
sbiSplitNo × sbiGodownId × sbiStockId), so posting a bill is a straight
INSERT ... SELECT with no reshaping and no aggregation. A transfer writes
TWO rows, not one: TRANSFER_OUT at the source and TRANSFER_IN at the
destination, each with its own godown — and they are not simultaneous, see
stock_transit.

THERE IS NO RUNNING-BALANCE COLUMN, on purpose. "Balance after this
movement" is the classic stock-ledger trap: two tills posting concurrently
must serialise on it, a back-dated entry invalidates every row after it,
and an offline chain has no global order to compute it in. The balance is
stock_balance\'s job and nothing else\'s.

FREE QUANTITY IS STOCK. Free goods sit on the shelf and get sold, so they
are counted in on-hand — but kept in their own columns, because margin
reporting must separate what was paid for from what was not.
smlSignedBaseQty adds both and applies the direction; it is the one column
every quantity report sums, and the only one stock_balance reads.

PARTITIONED BY LIST (sml_acc_year), hence the composite primary key
(smlId, smlAccYear). A new fiscal year needs its partitions created before
the first save of that year, or EVERY insert fails:
SELECT public.ensure_acc_year_partitions(\'2027-2028\');
That helper is the ONE place a year is opened — the same call that
provisions sale_bill, sale_bill_item, acc_tender_detail, txn_status_log and
the rest. It does NOT know about this table until the migration that
creates stock_ledger extends it (CREATE OR REPLACE, restating every
existing block in order, as 20260810140000 and 20260811080000 did), so
extending it is part of adding this table, not a follow-up.

WRITE RULES:
* NEVER write smlSignedBaseQty — GENERATED ALWAYS ... STORED, and
Postgres rejects any write to it.
* Quantities are MAGNITUDES; the sign lives in smlDirection alone. A
negative qty with a negative direction is a silent double negation, so
ck_sml_qty_sign refuses it.
* smlAccYear is this ROW\'s year; smlSrcAccYear is the DOCUMENT\'s, and
they differ legitimately — a sale return in April against a March bill
posts here in the new year.
* On an outward, the cost columns are what the stock cost US (the COGS),
taken from the lot or the moving average per stpValuationMethod —
NEVER the selling price. The selling price goes in smlDocRate.

Vocabularies, enforced by CHECK constraints in the migration:
smlTxnType    OPENING | PURCHASE | PURCHASE_RETURN | SALE | SALE_RETURN |
TRANSFER_OUT | TRANSFER_IN | ADJUST_PLUS | ADJUST_MINUS |
PHYSICAL_PLUS | PHYSICAL_MINUS | DAMAGE | EXPIRY_WRITEOFF |
REPACK_IN | REPACK_OUT | BUCKET_IN | BUCKET_OUT |
GIFT_ISSUE | SAMPLE_ISSUE
smlDirection  1 (in) | -1 (out) — redundant against smlTxnType on
purpose: a report that sums must not have to know that
EXPIRY_WRITEOFF is an out
smlBucket     SALEABLE | DAMAGED | QUARANTINE | EXPIRED | SAMPLE —
saleable stock and damaged stock are both stock, and must
never be added together by accident
smlSrcModule  SALES | PURCHASE | STOCK | LOYALTY | PRODUCTION | MIGRATION
plus ck_sml_acc_year (YYYY-YYYY, second = first + 1), ck_sml_qty_nonzero,
ck_sml_to_base_factor and ck_sml_reversal.

DB-only objects Prisma cannot express — hand-authored in the migration and
deliberately NOT declared here. EVERY index on this table carries a WHERE,
and Prisma ignores indexes with predicates:
ux_sml_source     POSTING IDEMPOTENCY. The same bill posted twice — a
retry after a timeout, a sync replaying a day — must
not move stock twice. Direction is in the key because a
transfer legitimately writes an OUT and an IN for one
source line. Partial on NOT deleted AND NOT reversal.
ux_sml_reversal   REVERSAL IDEMPOTENCY. ux_sml_source excludes reversal
rows, which would otherwise have no unique constraint
at all; fn_svh_cancel guards with a NOT EXISTS, but
that is read-then-write and two concurrent cancels
could both pass it and reverse the same movement twice.
ix_sml_item_date  the item ledger screen
ix_sml_balance_scope  fn_sbl_rebuild and every as-on-date recomputation
ix_sml_lot        lot movement history: the recall trail
ix_sml_source_doc drilling from a document into what it moved
ix_sml_demand     fn_sdd_rebuild: the sales-only slice, by day'
}

Table stock_lot {
  sltId String [pk]
  sltCompanyId String [not null]
  sltTenantId String
  sltItemId String [not null]
  sltBaseUomId String [not null, note: 'The item\'s BASE unit — an item_unit_conversion(iuc_id), NEVER an
item_unit_master(unit_id). Every quantity in this engine is stored in
this unit as well as in the unit it was keyed in.']
  sltBatchNo String
  sltMfgBatchNo String
  sltMrp Decimal
  sltSalePrice Decimal
  sltMfgDate DateTime
  sltExpiryDate DateTime
  sltSerialNo String
  sltSupplierId String
  sltKeyBatch String
  sltKeyMrp Decimal
  sltKeySp Decimal
  sltKeyExpiry DateTime
  sltKeySerial String
  sltKeySupplier String
  sltTrackSignature String [not null, default: 'N', note: 'Which StockTrackPolicy flags were live when this lot was created —
B/M/S/E/R/P, \'N\' when nothing is tracked. A policy change tomorrow must
not make yesterday\'s lots unreadable.']
  sltFirstInwardDate DateTime [not null, note: 'The ageing anchor for the CHAIN as a whole (StockTrackPolicy
stpAgeingBasis = INWARD_DATE reads this). A godown\'s own "how long has
this sat HERE" reads stock_balance.sbl_first_in_date instead.']
  sltFirstInwardBranch String
  sltInwardSrcModule String [note: 'The (module, docType, docId) triple, all-or-nothing per ck_slt_inward_src.']
  sltInwardSrcDocType String
  sltInwardSrcDocId String
  sltInwardSrcAccYear String
  sltInwardRefno String
  sltPurchaseRate Decimal [not null, default: 0, note: 'sltCostRate is THIS lot\'s own cost, which is what makes FIFO and
LOT_ACTUAL valuation a read rather than a replay. The moving weighted
average is maintained in parallel in stock_item_cost. Both, always, so
stpValuationMethod selects an answer instead of triggering a rebuild.']
  sltCostRate Decimal [not null, default: 0]
  sltCostRateWot Decimal [not null, default: 0]
  sltLandedRate Decimal [not null, default: 0]
  sltTaxRate Decimal [not null, default: 0]
  sltStatus String [not null, default: 'ACTIVE', note: 'CLOSED is set by the engine when the last unit leaves; BLOCKED and
EXPIRED are business decisions that stop the lot being issued.']
  sltClosedOn DateTime
  sltBlockedReason String
  sltTotalOnHand Decimal [not null, default: 0, note: 'Chain-wide total across every branch and godown. TRIGGER-MAINTAINED from
StockLedger by fn_sml_apply(). NEVER written from application code.']
  sltRowVersion BigInt [not null, default: 1]
  sltRemarks String
  sltIsActive Boolean [not null, default: true]
  sltIsDeleted Boolean [not null, default: false]
  sltSyncDate DateTime
  sltCreatedOn DateTime [default: `now()`, not null]
  sltCreatedBy String
  sltModifiedOn DateTime
  sltModifiedBy String
  company companys [not null]
  item item_master [not null]
  baseUom item_unit_conversion [not null]
  supplier suppliers
  firstInwardBranch branch_master
  ledgerEntries stock_ledger [not null]
  balances stock_balance [not null]
  voucherItems stock_voucher_item [not null]
  reservations stock_reservation [not null]
  transits stock_transit [not null]
  valuationSnaps stock_valuation_snap [not null]
  saleReturnItems sale_return_item [not null]
  saleDcItems sale_dc_item [not null]
  saleDcReturnItems sale_dc_return_item [not null]
  saleBillItems sale_bill_item [not null]

  Note: 'stock.stock_lot — the IDENTITY of a holding. Never its quantity.

A lot is "this item, this batch, this MRP, this selling price, this expiry,
from this supplier". Quantity lives in stock_balance, movement in
StockLedger. Splitting identity from quantity is what lets the same lot be
held in four godowns across three branches without four truths about its
expiry date.

COMPANY-SCOPED, DELIBERATELY. A lot is NOT per branch. Transfer stock from
one branch to another and it is the same batch, the same expiry, the same
supplier and — critically — the same AGE. A per-branch lot would reset the
ageing clock at every transfer and let a chain hide ninety-day-old stock by
moving it. Branch and godown live on stock_balance, which carries its own
sbl_first_in_date for "how long has it been on THIS shelf".

THE NULL-NORMALISED KEY. Every tracked dimension is a nullable column with a
GENERATED companion that substitutes a sentinel when it is NULL:

tracked:      sltMrp = 120.00  →  sltKeyMrp = 120.00
not tracked:  sltMrp = NULL    →  sltKeyMrp = -1

ux_slt_identity is built over the COMPANIONS, never the originals, because
a UNIQUE index treats every NULL as distinct — which would let the same
untracked item accrete a new lot on every single receipt, for ever. So an
item tracked by nothing has exactly ONE lot with all six identity columns
NULL, and there is no "non-batch path" anywhere in the engine.

Sentinels are impossible values, not merely unlikely: \'~\' sorts after every
alphanumeric batch number, -1 cannot be a price, 0001-01-01 cannot be an
expiry, and the nil uuid is not a supplier.

SUPPLIER IS ON THE LOT, not derived by tracing consumption back to a
purchase. The same batch number received from two suppliers is two lots.
That costs a few more rows and buys exact supplier-wise stock, exact
supplier-wise ageing, and a recall that names the right supplier without a
single join back through the ledger.

WRITE RULES:
* NEVER write sltKeyBatch / sltKeyMrp / sltKeySp / sltKeyExpiry /
sltKeySerial / sltKeySupplier — all six are GENERATED ALWAYS ... STORED
and Postgres rejects any write to them. Write the nullable originals.
* NEVER write sltTotalOnHand. It is TRIGGER-MAINTAINED from StockLedger
by fn_sml_apply(); stock_balance owns the per-godown truth it sums.
* sltTrackSignature is stamped from the resolved StockTrackPolicy at
creation, so a policy change tomorrow cannot re-key yesterday\'s stock.

Vocabularies, enforced by CHECK constraints in the migration:
sltStatus  ACTIVE | CLOSED | BLOCKED | EXPIRED
plus ck_slt_expiry_order, ck_slt_prices, ck_slt_batch_shape,
ck_slt_serial_shape and ck_slt_inward_src (the source triple is
all-or-nothing, as everywhere else in the chain).

DB-only objects Prisma cannot express — hand-authored in the migration and
deliberately NOT declared here (every one is partial, and ux_slt_identity
is over generated columns as well):
ux_slt_identity  THE index of the stock engine — the one that collapses
batch-wise, MRP-wise, sale-price-wise and none into one
mechanism
ix_slt_expiry    FEFO and near-expiry
ix_slt_inward    FIFO / LIFO, and the chain-wide ageing report
ix_slt_supplier  supplier-wise stock and supplier recall
ix_slt_batch_no  "who has any of batch AX-4471?"
ix_slt_mrp       "how much at 120, how much at the old 110"'
}

Table stock_movement_class {
  smcId String [pk]
  smcCompanyId String [not null]
  smcBranchId String [not null]
  smcTenantId String
  smcItemId String [not null]
  smcPeriodFrom DateTime [not null, note: 'Explicit, and part of the unique key, so monthly and quarterly runs
coexist without overwriting each other.']
  smcPeriodTo DateTime [not null]
  smcAbcClass String [note: 'By VALUE. NULL = no movement in the period, which is not the same claim
as a \'C\'.']
  smcXyzClass String [note: 'By VARIABILITY — an X can be forecast, a Z can only be buffered.']
  smcFsnClass String [note: 'By FREQUENCY — Fast, Slow, Non-moving.']
  smcVelocityQtyPerDay Decimal [not null, default: 0]
  smcMovementCount Int [not null, default: 0]
  smcDaysSinceLastSale Int
  smcDaysOfCover Decimal
  smcTurnRatio Decimal [note: 'COGS in the period over average stock held: how many times the shelf
turned over. The number a retailer actually manages by.']
  smcDemandCv Decimal [note: 'Coefficient of variation — the figure the XYZ class is cut from, kept
beside the letter so the cut can be re-examined.']
  smcPeriodQty Decimal [not null, default: 0]
  smcPeriodValueWot Decimal [not null, default: 0]
  smcAvgStockQty Decimal [not null, default: 0]
  smcAvgStockValue Decimal [not null, default: 0]
  smcClosingQty Decimal [not null, default: 0]
  smcClosingValue Decimal [not null, default: 0]
  smcRankInBranch Int [note: 'The working of the ABC cut, stored so the A/B boundary can be audited
rather than reproduced.']
  smcCumValuePerc Decimal
  smcDeadStockFlag Boolean [not null, default: false, note: 'Held, not sold, for long enough to be a decision.']
  smcDeadSinceDate DateTime [note: 'Required whenever the flag is set — "dead since November" is actionable,
"dead" is not.']
  smcDeadValue Decimal [not null, default: 0, note: 'What is tied up. Makes the liquidation worklist sortable by how much it
hurts.']
  smcComputedOn DateTime [default: `now()`, not null]
  smcIsDeleted Boolean [not null, default: false]
  smcSyncDate DateTime
  smcCreatedOn DateTime [default: `now()`, not null]
  company companys [not null]
  branch branch_master [not null]
  item item_master [not null]

  Note: 'stock.stock_movement_class — fast, slow, dead.

THREE ORTHOGONAL CLASSIFICATIONS, because they answer different questions
and a single "category A" hides two of them:

ABC  by VALUE        where the money is. 20% of items, 80% of value.
XYZ  by VARIABILITY  how predictable. An X item can be FORECAST; a Z item
can only be BUFFERED.
FSN  by FREQUENCY    how often it moves at all. Fast, Slow, Non-moving.

An A-Z item — high value, wildly unpredictable — is the one that ties up
capital and STILL stocks out, and it is invisible to any single ranking.
That combination is the whole reason there are three columns and not one.

COMPUTED PER PERIOD AND STORED, not derived on read. The classification of
March must not change when April\'s sales arrive: a buyer who was told an
item was dead in March needs that to still be what the system said in
March. Period bounds are explicit — smcPeriodFrom / smcPeriodTo — and are
part of the unique key, so monthly and quarterly runs coexist.

smcTurnRatio is cost of goods sold in the period over average stock held:
how many times the shelf turned over, and the number a retailer actually
manages by. smcDemandCv is the coefficient of variation the XYZ class is
cut from, kept beside the letter so the cut can be re-examined without
recomputing the period.

DEAD STOCK NEEDS A DATE. "Dead since November" is actionable — it dates the
buying mistake and prices the liquidation; "dead" alone is not.
ck_smc_dead therefore refuses a dead flag with no smcDeadSinceDate, and
smcDeadValue is what is tied up, which is what makes the worklist sortable
by how much it hurts.

smcRankInBranch and smcCumValuePerc are the working of the ABC cut, stored
so the boundary between A and B can be audited rather than reproduced.

WRITE RULES:
* This table is DERIVED, from [[StockLedger]] and [[StockValuationSnap]].
Do not patch a row to change a class; fix the inputs and recompute.
* The three class columns are NULLABLE — an item with no movement in the
period has no honest class, and NULL says that where \'N\' would be a
claim.

Vocabularies, enforced by CHECK constraints in the migration:
smcAbcClass  A | B | C     (by value)
smcXyzClass  X | Y | Z     (by variability)
smcFsnClass  F | S | N     (by frequency)
each nullable, plus ck_smc_period (to >= from) and ck_smc_dead.

DB-only objects Prisma cannot express — hand-authored in the migration and
deliberately NOT declared here (all three carry a WHERE):
ux_smc_scope  one row per (company, branch, item, period)
ix_smc_class  the classification report, and the A-Z drilldown
ix_smc_dead   dead stock, worst first — the liquidation worklist'
}

Table stock_reason_master {
  srmId String [pk]
  srmCompanyId String [note: 'NULL = SHARED with every company; a company row with the same code
overrides the shared one in the picker.']
  srmCode String [not null]
  srmName String [not null]
  srmAllowedTxnTypes String[] [not null, note: 'Which movements may cite it. Empty array = any. Kept as text[] rather
than a child table because it is a filter for a dropdown, not a rule
anybody reports on. NEVER null — see WRITE RULES above.']
  srmDirection String [not null, default: 'BOTH', note: 'IN, OUT or BOTH — stops "damaged" appearing on a receipt screen.']
  srmRequireRemarks Boolean [not null, default: false]
  srmGlLedgerId String [note: 'Where the value goes when stock is written off. NULL = no auto posting.']
  srmSortOrder Int [not null, default: 0]
  srmRemarks String
  srmIsActive Boolean [not null, default: true]
  srmIsDeleted Boolean [not null, default: false]
  srmSyncDate DateTime
  srmCreatedOn DateTime [default: `now()`, not null]
  srmCreatedBy String
  srmModifiedOn DateTime
  srmModifiedBy String
  company companys
  glLedger acc_ledger_master
  ledgerEntries stock_ledger [not null]
  vouchers stock_voucher [not null]
  voucherItems stock_voucher_item [not null]
  saleReturns sale_return [not null]
  saleDcReturns sale_dc_return [not null]

  Note: 'stock.stock_reason_master — WHY stock moved when no trade document explains
it. A receipt says "we bought it" and an invoice says "we sold it"; an
ADJUSTMENT says nothing on its own, so it must cite a row from here.

This table is LOAD-BEARING, not a lookup for a dropdown label:
* fn_svh_txn_map REFUSES an ADJUSTMENT that cites no reason, and refuses
one whose reason is direction BOTH — a document that cannot say which
way it moved is not a document. With this table empty, ADJUSTMENT
cannot be posted at all.
* srmDirection (IN | OUT) is what turns an ADJUSTMENT into ADJUST_PLUS or
ADJUST_MINUS. BOTH is only safe where the direction comes from
elsewhere — a PHYSICAL count, where the counted difference supplies it
— so every BOTH row is restricted to PHYSICAL via srmAllowedTxnTypes.
* srmAllowedTxnTypes empty = any movement may cite it. But when a reason
names EXACTLY ONE type and the voucher is an ISSUE, fn_svh_txn_map
BELIEVES the reason and posts that type: an ISSUE citing SAMPLE becomes
SAMPLE_ISSUE, not a bare ADJUST_MINUS. That is the whole mechanism by
which sample, gift and internal-use issues classify themselves.

COMPANY SCOPING is a MERGE, and this is the one place in the stock module
where scoping merges. The tracking policy picks the MOST SPECIFIC row, the
ageing ladder is ALL-OR-NOTHING, but reasons ADD UP: srmCompanyId NULL =
SHARED with every company, and the picker shows the shared rows and the
company\'s own together, because the user is choosing one from a list and
more choices are not a contradiction. A company that defines its own row
with a shared row\'s code OVERRIDES it — the picker hides the shared one.
Nothing is copied per company. Same rule as [[StockTrackPreset]].

srmGlLedgerId is where the value of written-off stock lands. NULL = no auto
posting, and the seed leaves every row NULL because the chart of accounts
is a per-company question. Nothing in the stock engine reads it yet.

WRITE RULES:
* srmCode is the stable identity, unique per company (or per SHARED),
[A-Za-z0-9_-] only — enforced by ck_srm_code_shape.
* srmAllowedTxnTypes is a Postgres text[] with DEFAULT \'{}\'. Prisma
scalar lists REJECT null and report it as a missing-relation error, so
normalise an absent value to [] in the DTO — never pass null.

Vocabulary, enforced by a CHECK constraint in the migration:
srmDirection  IN | OUT | BOTH

DB-only objects Prisma cannot express — hand-authored in the migration and
deliberately NOT declared here (both carry a WHERE, and Prisma ignores
indexes with predicates):
ux_srm_code   unique on (COALESCE(company, nil uuid), code) WHERE NOT
deleted — COALESCEd because a plain unique index treats
every NULL as distinct, which would let the seed insert
SHORTAGE twice. Uniqueness on this path is app-side.
ix_srm_list   the picker\'s list shape, partial on active + not deleted.'
}

Table stock_reorder_policy {
  srpId String [pk]
  srpCompanyId String [not null]
  srpBranchId String [not null]
  srpGodownId String [note: 'NULL = the whole branch, however many godowns it operates.']
  srpItemId String [not null]
  srpUomId String [not null, note: 'The unit the buyer thinks in — an item_unit_conversion(iuc_id). Every
quantity below is in THIS unit, not the base unit.']
  srpMinQty Decimal [not null, default: 0]
  srpMaxQty Decimal [not null, default: 0, note: '0 = NO CEILING, exempted from the "max >= min" rule.']
  srpReorderLevel Decimal [not null, default: 0]
  srpReorderQty Decimal [not null, default: 0]
  srpSafetyStock Decimal [not null, default: 0]
  srpLeadTimeDays Decimal [not null, default: 0]
  srpLeadTimeSdDays Decimal [not null, default: 0, note: 'The standard deviation of the lead time — what tells "3 days ± 1" apart
from "3 days ± 6", and the only thing that makes safety stock honest.']
  srpReviewPeriodDays Int [not null, default: 7]
  srpServiceLevelPerc Decimal [not null, default: 95, note: 'The probability of not stocking out during lead time that the safety
stock is sized for. 95.000 is the usual retail answer; strictly between
0 and 100, because 100% service means infinite stock.']
  srpMoq Decimal [not null, default: 0, note: 'The supplier\'s constraints, applied AFTER the maths.']
  srpPackMultiple Decimal [not null, default: 1]
  srpShelfCapacityQty Decimal [not null, default: 0, note: 'The opposite constraint: no point ordering more than the shelf holds.']
  srpPreferredSupplierId String
  srpSource String [not null, default: 'MANUAL', note: 'MANUAL means a human set these and a job MUST NOT overwrite them.']
  srpComputedOn DateTime
  srpAutoReplenish Boolean [not null, default: false, note: 'Arms the sweep that raises orders without a human. Off by default.']
  srpRemarks String
  srpIsActive Boolean [not null, default: true]
  srpIsDeleted Boolean [not null, default: false]
  srpSyncDate DateTime
  srpCreatedOn DateTime [default: `now()`, not null]
  srpCreatedBy String
  srpModifiedOn DateTime
  srpModifiedBy String
  company companys [not null]
  branch branch_master [not null]
  godown godown_locations
  item item_master [not null]
  uom item_unit_conversion [not null]
  preferredSupplier suppliers

  Note: 'stock.stock_reorder_policy — the SETTINGS a replenishment decision is made
from. One row per item per location.

srpGodownId NULL = THE WHOLE BRANCH, however many godowns it operates. A
shop keeps one reorder level for an item and does not care whether the
units are on the shelf or in the back store; a warehouse that does care
adds a godown-specific row beside it. ux_srp_scope COALESCEs the godown to
the nil uuid, because a plain unique index treats every NULL as distinct
and would let the same branch-wide row be inserted twice.

srpSource IS LOAD-BEARING, not a label. MANUAL means a human set these
numbers and the nightly job MUST NOT overwrite them: a buyer who has
deliberately raised a level for a festival will not do it twice, and a job
that silently reverts it destroys trust in the whole module. COMPUTED rows
are the job\'s to maintain, and srpComputedOn says when it last did.

THE NUMBERS ARE SETTINGS, NOT ANSWERS. What to order today is
[[StockForecast]].sfcSuggestedOrderQty, computed from these plus actual
demand. This table holds only what a human decided about the item: how low
it may go, how much to buy, how long the supplier takes, and how sure we
want to be.

srpServiceLevelPerc is the probability of NOT stocking out during lead
time that srpSafetyStock is sized for. 95.000 is the usual retail answer,
and it is strictly between 0 and 100 — 100% service means infinite safety
stock, which ck_srp_service_level refuses rather than letting a job compute
it.

srpLeadTimeSdDays is the standard deviation of the lead time, and it is
what makes safety stock honest: an item whose supplier takes 3 days ± 1 and
an item whose supplier takes 3 days ± 6 need very different buffers, and
only the second figure tells them apart.

srpMoq and srpPackMultiple are the supplier\'s constraints, applied AFTER
the maths — a suggestion of 17 against a case of 12 becomes 24, and
pretending otherwise produces orders no supplier will accept.
srpShelfCapacityQty is the opposite constraint: no point ordering more than
the shelf holds.

WRITE RULES:
* srpUomId is part of the key: min/max/reorder are expressed in the unit
a buyer thinks in ("reorder at 4 cases"), NOT in the base unit.
* A max of 0 means NO CEILING — ck_srp_levels exempts it from the
"max >= min" rule rather than forcing a sentinel.
* srpAutoReplenish arms the sweep that raises orders without a human. It
is off by default, and it should stay off until the forecast for that
item has been watched for a while.

Vocabulary, enforced by a CHECK constraint in the migration:
srpSource  MANUAL | COMPUTED
plus ck_srp_levels, ck_srp_lead_time, ck_srp_service_level, ck_srp_pack
(a pack multiple of 0 would divide by zero) and ck_srp_review.

DB-only objects Prisma cannot express — hand-authored in the migration and
deliberately NOT declared here (all three carry a WHERE, and ux_srp_scope
is over an expression as well):
ux_srp_scope  one policy per item per location, godown COALESCEd to the
nil uuid so the branch-wide row has something to be unique
against. Uniqueness on this path is app-side.
ix_srp_item   the item\'s policies across branches
ix_srp_auto   the auto-replenishment sweep'
}

Table stock_reservation {
  srvId String [not null]
  srvCompanyId String [not null]
  srvBranchId String [not null]
  srvTenantId String
  srvAccYear String [not null, note: 'The PARTITION KEY, and half the primary key.']
  srvGodownId String [not null]
  srvItemId String [not null]
  srvLotId String [not null]
  srvBaseUomId String [not null]
  srvBucket String [not null, default: 'SALEABLE']
  srvSrcModule String [not null]
  srvSrcDocType String [not null]
  srvSrcDocId String [not null]
  srvSrcAccYear String
  srvSrcRefno String
  srvLineNo Int [not null, default: 1]
  srvReservedQty Decimal [not null, default: 0]
  srvConsumedQty Decimal [not null, default: 0]
  srvReleasedQty Decimal [not null, default: 0]
  srvOpenQty Decimal [note: 'GENERATED ALWAYS ... STORED as reserved - consumed - released. What is
still holding stock, and what the trigger sums into
StockBalance.sblReservedQty. Read-only, never sent.']
  srvReservedOn DateTime [default: `now()`, not null]
  srvExpiresOn DateTime [note: 'Without this, an abandoned order holds stock for ever.']
  srvStatus String [not null, default: 'OPEN']
  srvClosedOn DateTime
  srvCloseReason String
  srvIsDeleted Boolean [not null, default: false]
  srvSyncDate DateTime
  srvCreatedOn DateTime [default: `now()`, not null]
  srvCreatedBy String
  srvModifiedOn DateTime
  srvModifiedBy String
  company companys [not null]
  branch branch_master [not null]
  godown godown_locations [not null]
  item item_master [not null]
  lot stock_lot [not null]
  baseUom item_unit_conversion [not null]

  indexes {
    (srvId, srvAccYear) [pk]
  }

  Note: 'stock.stock_reservation — quantity PROMISED but still on the shelf.

A sale order taken at one till against stock a second till is about to
sell. Counting open orders out of SaleOrderItem cannot work in a chain: the
order that committed the last unit may have been raised offline in another
branch and not yet synced, and soiPendingQty is a cache of the ORDER\'s
state, not of the STOCK\'s. So the hold lives beside the stock it holds.

RESERVED STOCK IS ON HAND. It is physically there — a physical count will
find it, a valuation must include it — but it is NOT AVAILABLE. That
distinction is exactly why [[StockBalance]] carries sblOnHandQty and
sblAvailableQty as separate figures, and a billing screen must check the
second.

A RESERVATION EXPIRES, and srvExpiresOn is not optional in spirit: without
it a quoted order abandoned in March holds stock hostage for ever, and the
only cure is a human noticing. The expiry sweep reads ix_srv_expiring.

THREE COUNTERS, ONE ANSWER. srvReservedQty is what was promised,
srvConsumedQty is what the eventual document actually took, srvReleasedQty
is what was given back — and srvOpenQty, GENERATED from all three, is what
is still holding stock. The trigger sums srvOpenQty into
StockBalance.sblReservedQty; nothing else may write that column.
ck_srv_settled refuses consuming and releasing more than was ever reserved.

A PARTIAL is a reservation whose document took some of it. CONSUMED,
RELEASED and EXPIRED are terminal, and a terminal row keeps its numbers
rather than being deleted — "we held four units for that order and the
customer never came" is a question somebody asks.

WRITE RULES:
* NEVER write srvOpenQty — GENERATED ALWAYS ... STORED.
* NEVER write StockBalance.sblReservedQty; write here and let the trigger
aggregate.
* One reservation per source line per lot — ux_srv_source — so a re-post
UPDATES rather than stacking a second hold on the same stock. Getting
this wrong double-reserves and makes stock vanish from the sale screen.

Vocabularies, enforced by CHECK constraints in the migration:
srvStatus  OPEN | PARTIAL | CONSUMED | RELEASED | EXPIRED
srvBucket  SALEABLE | DAMAGED | QUARANTINE | EXPIRED | SAMPLE
plus ck_srv_acc_year and ck_srv_qty (a reservation of zero is not a
reservation).

PARTITIONED BY LIST (srv_acc_year), hence the composite primary key.

DB-only objects Prisma cannot express — hand-authored in the migration and
deliberately NOT declared here (every one carries a WHERE):
ux_srv_source   one hold per source line per lot
ix_srv_balance  the trigger\'s aggregate: open holds against one balance
ix_srv_expiring the expiry sweep'
}

Table stock_track_policy {
  stpId String [pk]
  stpCompanyId String [note: 'NULL = EVERY company. item_master.item_company_id is itself nullable and
items are deliberately not company-owned, so a company-less item would
have nothing to write a policy against. A company row beats a NULL one.']
  stpBranchId String [note: 'NULL = every branch in the company. A branch row beats a company row.']
  stpScope String [not null, default: 'COMPANY']
  stpScopeId String [note: 'NULL only when stpScope = \'COMPANY\'.']
  stpItemId String
  stpGroupId String
  stpTrackBatch Boolean [not null, default: false]
  stpTrackMrp Boolean [not null, default: false]
  stpTrackSalePrice Boolean [not null, default: false]
  stpTrackExpiry Boolean [not null, default: false]
  stpTrackSerial Boolean [not null, default: false]
  stpTrackSupplier Boolean [not null, default: false, note: 'Tracked separately from batch on purpose: the same batch number from two
suppliers is two holdings, and only this flag decides whether the
business cares.']
  stpTrackSignature String [note: 'GENERATED ALWAYS ... STORED. B/M/S/E/R/P, \'N\' when nothing is tracked.']
  stpValuationMethod String [not null, default: 'WAVG', note: 'WAVG reads stock_item_cost; FIFO and LOT_ACTUAL read slt_cost_rate. Both
are always maintained, so this only selects which one a report believes —
changing it needs no recomputation.']
  stpIssueStrategy String [not null, default: 'FEFO', note: 'The single most consequential column here. /master-lookups/item-price
returns ONE stock row to the billing screen; before this there was
nothing in the schema that said which. FEFO is the default because it is
right for anything perishable and harmless for anything else.']
  stpAllowNegative String [not null, default: 'ALLOW', note: 'ALLOW because a counter must be able to sell before the GRN clerk has
caught up. WARN posts and flags. BLOCK aborts the transaction — for
controlled substances and anything serialised.']
  stpShelfLifeDays Int
  stpNearExpiryDays Int [not null, default: 30]
  stpBlockExpiredSale Boolean [not null, default: false]
  stpAgeingBasis String [not null, default: 'INWARD_DATE', note: 'INWARD_DATE  — when the lot first entered the company (chain-wide age)
LAST_IN_DATE — when it last entered THIS godown (shelf age)
MFG_DATE     — age of the goods themselves']
  stpEffectiveFrom DateTime [not null]
  stpEffectiveTo DateTime [not null]
  stpRemarks String
  stpIsActive Boolean [not null, default: true]
  stpIsDeleted Boolean [not null, default: false]
  stpSyncDate DateTime
  stpCreatedOn DateTime [default: `now()`, not null]
  stpCreatedBy String
  stpModifiedOn DateTime
  stpModifiedBy String
  company companys
  branch branch_master
  item item_master
  itemGroup item_group_master

  Note: 'stock.stock_track_policy — how a holding is keyed, valued, issued and aged.

One row answers, for a (company, branch, scope) at a document\'s date: what
makes two holdings of the same item DIFFERENT (batch / mrp / sale price /
expiry / serial / supplier), which one goes out when nobody names a lot,
which cost a report believes, and whether the counter may sell into
negative stock.

RESOLUTION is most-specific-first, and the window is resolved against the
DOCUMENT\'s date, not today\'s — a business can genuinely say "we start
tracking batches on 1 July" and have June\'s receipts keep the identity
scheme they were entered under:

branch + ITEM  →  branch + GROUP  →  branch + COMPANY
company + ITEM →  company + GROUP →  company + COMPANY
(stpCompanyId NULL = every company; stpBranchId NULL = every branch)

stpEffectiveFrom defaults to 1900-01-01 on purpose. Defaulting it to today
looks tidier and is a trap: a policy authored today would not apply to a
receipt back-dated to yesterday, which would silently fall through to
"track nothing" and create an untracked lot for an item the business
believes is batch-tracked. A policy with no stated start date has ALWAYS
been in force.

WRITE RULES:
* set stpScope + stpScopeId; NEVER write stpItemId / stpGroupId /
stpTrackSignature — all three are GENERATED ALWAYS ... STORED and
Postgres rejects any write to them.
* stpScopeId is NULL only when stpScope = \'COMPANY\'.

stpTrackSignature is B/M/S/E/R/P in that order, \'N\' when nothing is
tracked. It is stamped onto every lot at creation so history survives a
policy change.

Vocabularies, enforced by CHECK constraints in the migration:
stpScope            COMPANY | GROUP | ITEM
stpValuationMethod  WAVG | FIFO | LOT_ACTUAL
stpIssueStrategy    FIFO | FEFO | LIFO | MANUAL
stpAllowNegative    ALLOW | WARN | BLOCK
stpAgeingBasis      INWARD_DATE | MFG_DATE | LAST_IN_DATE
plus ck_stp_expiry_needs_batch (expiry without batch cannot be keyed) and
ck_stp_fefo_needs_expiry (FEFO can only order lots that have an expiry).

DB-only objects Prisma cannot express — hand-authored in the migration and
deliberately NOT declared here:
ex_stp_overlap   EXCLUDE USING gist — two policies for the same thing,
same branch, overlapping dates, is not a rule but a
race. Needs btree_gist. NULL company/branch/scope_id are
COALESCEd to the nil uuid because EXCLUDE never
conflicts on NULL.
ix_stp_resolve   the resolver\'s exact lookup shape, covering, partial
ix_stp_item      reverse lookup / FK-covering, partial'
}

Table stock_track_preset {
  sptId String [pk]
  sptCompanyId String [note: 'NULL = shared with every company; a company row with the same code
overrides the shared one in the picker.']
  sptCode String [not null]
  sptName String [not null, note: 'Named by TRADE, not by flags: "Pharma" tells the user more than
"batch+expiry+MRP+supplier" ever will. The flags live in the tooltip.']
  sptDescription String
  sptTrackBatch Boolean [not null, default: false]
  sptTrackMrp Boolean [not null, default: false]
  sptTrackSalePrice Boolean [not null, default: false]
  sptTrackExpiry Boolean [not null, default: false]
  sptTrackSerial Boolean [not null, default: false]
  sptTrackSupplier Boolean [not null, default: false]
  sptTrackSignature String [note: 'GENERATED ALWAYS ... STORED, same expression as stpTrackSignature so a
screen can match a saved policy back to a preset by comparing the two.']
  sptValuationMethod String [not null, default: 'WAVG']
  sptIssueStrategy String [not null, default: 'FEFO']
  sptAllowNegative String [not null, default: 'ALLOW']
  sptShelfLifeDays Int
  sptNearExpiryDays Int [not null, default: 30]
  sptBlockExpiredSale Boolean [not null, default: false]
  sptAgeingBasis String [not null, default: 'INWARD_DATE']
  sptSortOrder Int [not null, default: 0]
  sptRemarks String
  sptIsActive Boolean [not null, default: true]
  sptIsDeleted Boolean [not null, default: false]
  sptSyncDate DateTime
  sptCreatedOn DateTime [default: `now()`, not null]
  sptCreatedBy String
  sptModifiedOn DateTime
  sptModifiedBy String
  company companys
  items item_master [not null, note: 'The masters configured with this preset. Back-relations only — a preset
still has NO relation to StockTrackPolicy, and must not gain one.']
  itemGroups item_group_master [not null]

  Note: 'stock.stock_track_preset — named tracking-flag combinations, offered by the
policy screen as a combo of TRADES ("Pharma", "Perishables") instead of six
checkboxes. Adding a preset for a new trade is an INSERT, not a release.

THE ENGINE NEVER READS THIS TABLE. fn_stp_effective, fn_slt_resolve and
everything downstream read StockTrackPolicy\'s own six flags. A preset is a
STENCIL:

picking one COPIES its values into the policy form;
Save writes an ordinary StockTrackPolicy row that owns its flags;
after that the preset is irrelevant to that policy for ever.

There is deliberately NO relation from StockTrackPolicy to this model and
no sptId stored on the policy. Link semantics — where editing a preset
re-keys every policy that used it — would silently re-identify stock across
every company at a stroke: exactly the accident the policy\'s effective
dates and the lot\'s stamped signature exist to prevent. To show "which
preset is this policy?", match on the GENERATED signature
(stpTrackSignature = sptTrackSignature); no match means "Custom", which is
the truth.

WHAT THE MASTERS DO STORE. ItemMaster.itemTrackPresetId and
ItemGroupMaster.itgTrackPresetId (20260907060000) point HERE, and
StockTrackPolicyService copies this row\'s values into the policy when either
master is saved. That is not the link semantics refused above: the link is on
the MASTER, the policy row still owns its own thirteen columns outright, and
editing a preset changes no policy until somebody explicitly re-saves the
item or group that names it. What it buys is that a re-save re-derives the
SAME policy rather than falling back to the item\'s flags and quietly undoing
the choice. A policy still has no way back to a preset except the signature.

COMPANY SCOPING is a MERGE, exactly like the stock adjustment reasons:
sptCompanyId NULL = a SHARED preset offered to every company; a company row
with a shared row\'s code overrides it in the picker. Nothing has to be
copied per company.

The CHECK constraints are the POLICY\'s rulebook verbatim, on purpose: a
preset that cannot be saved as a policy is a trap waiting in a combo, so an
admin cannot author "Batch, FEFO, no expiry" here either.

WRITE RULES:
* NEVER write sptTrackSignature — it is GENERATED ALWAYS ... STORED and
Postgres rejects any write to it.
* sptCode is the stable identity, uppercase-ish [A-Za-z0-9_-] only.

Vocabularies, enforced by CHECK constraints in the migration:
sptValuationMethod  WAVG | FIFO | LOT_ACTUAL
sptIssueStrategy    FIFO | FEFO | LIFO | MANUAL
sptAllowNegative    ALLOW | WARN | BLOCK
sptAgeingBasis      INWARD_DATE | MFG_DATE | LAST_IN_DATE
plus ck_spt_expiry_needs_batch and ck_spt_fefo_needs_expiry — the same two
derivable rules the policy enforces.

DB-only objects Prisma cannot express — hand-authored in the migration and
deliberately NOT declared here (both carry a WHERE, and Prisma ignores
indexes with predicates):
ux_spt_code   unique on (COALESCE(company, nil uuid), code) WHERE NOT
deleted — COALESCEd because a plain unique index treats
every NULL as distinct, which would let the seed insert
PHARMA twice. Uniqueness on this path is app-side.
ix_spt_list   the picker\'s list shape, partial on active + not deleted.'
}

Table stock_transit {
  sttId String [pk]
  sttCompanyId String [not null]
  sttTenantId String
  sttFromBranchId String [not null]
  sttFromGodownId String [not null]
  sttToBranchId String [not null]
  sttToGodownId String [not null]
  sttItemId String [not null]
  sttLotId String [not null]
  sttBaseUomId String [not null]
  sttBucket String [not null, default: 'SALEABLE', note: 'In ux_stt_out_line on purpose — see ONE ROW PER above.']
  sttOutVoucherId String [not null]
  sttOutAccYear String [not null]
  sttOutRefno String
  sttInVoucherId String
  sttInAccYear String
  sttInRefno String
  sttSentQty Decimal [not null, default: 0]
  sttReceivedQty Decimal [not null, default: 0]
  sttDamageQty Decimal [not null, default: 0]
  sttShortQty Decimal [note: 'GENERATED ALWAYS ... STORED as sent - received - damage. What left and
never arrived, and the number a transfer-loss report is built on.
Read-only, never sent — it must not be editable.']
  sttCostRate Decimal [not null, default: 0]
  sttTransitValue Decimal [not null, default: 0]
  sttSentOn DateTime [default: `now()`, not null]
  sttExpectedOn DateTime
  sttReceivedOn DateTime
  sttStatus String [not null, default: 'IN_TRANSIT']
  sttLrNo String
  sttVehicleNo String
  sttRemarks String
  sttIsDeleted Boolean [not null, default: false]
  sttSyncDate DateTime
  sttCreatedOn DateTime [default: `now()`, not null]
  sttCreatedBy String
  sttModifiedOn DateTime
  sttModifiedBy String
  company companys [not null]
  fromBranch branch_master [not null]
  toBranch branch_master [not null]
  fromGodown godown_locations [not null]
  toGodown godown_locations [not null]
  item item_master [not null]
  lot stock_lot [not null]
  baseUom item_unit_conversion [not null]

  Note: 'stock.stock_transit — sent from A, not yet arrived at B.

A chain moves stock between stores constantly, and a transfer is not
instantaneous. Between the lorry leaving and the goods being checked in the
stock is out of A\'s balance and not yet in B\'s — and if nothing records it,
it is simply MISSING. That is how a chain loses stock on paper and then
finds it on the floor, and it is why this table exists rather than a status
flag on the transfer voucher.

TRANSIT IS NOT ON HAND. StockBalance.sblTransitInQty is fed from here and
is deliberately excluded from sblOnHandQty: it is not here yet, it cannot
be sold, and a physical count will not find it. It is shown so the
receiving branch knows what is coming, never so it can be billed.

NOT PARTITIONED, and this is the point. A transfer despatched on 29 March
and received on 2 April spans two accounting years; partitioning by year
would put the two halves of ONE fact in two partitions and make "what is in
transit right now" a query across years. Both voucher references therefore
carry their OWN year — sttOutAccYear and sttInAccYear — and neither carries
a foreign key, because a composite FK into a partitioned parent would pin
this row to one year\'s partition and defeat the whole arrangement. Same
reasoning as [[StockBalance]] and acc_bill_balance.

SHORT RECEIPTS ARE THE POINT. sttShortQty is what left and never arrived —
GENERATED, not entered, so it cannot be quietly rounded away by whoever
keys the receipt — and it is the number a transfer-loss report is built on.
Damage is tracked separately from shortage because they are different
failures with different people to talk to.

ONE ROW PER (out voucher, item, lot, destination godown, BUCKET). Bucket is
in the key because one voucher legitimately sends saleable units and
damaged units of the same lot to the same godown as two transit lines — a
returns-to-warehouse run does exactly this — and they must not collide.

WRITE RULES:
* NEVER write sttShortQty — GENERATED ALWAYS ... STORED.
* ck_stt_qty refuses receiving more than was sent; ck_stt_endpoints
refuses stock in transit to the place it already is.
* sttInVoucherId and sttInAccYear move together (ck_stt_in_pair).

Vocabularies, enforced by CHECK constraints in the migration:
sttStatus  IN_TRANSIT | PARTIAL | RECEIVED | CANCELLED
sttBucket  SALEABLE | DAMAGED | QUARANTINE | EXPIRED | SAMPLE

DB-only objects Prisma cannot express — hand-authored in the migration and
deliberately NOT declared here (every one carries a WHERE):
ux_stt_out_line  one line per (out voucher, item, lot, to-godown, bucket)
ix_stt_inbound   "what is on its way to me?" — the receiving worklist,
and the source of sblTransitInQty
ix_stt_outbound  "what have I sent that has not been acknowledged?" — the
ageing of transit itself, where losses show up'
}

Table stock_valuation_snap {
  svsId String [pk]
  svsCompanyId String [not null]
  svsBranchId String [not null]
  svsTenantId String
  svsAccYear String [not null, note: 'A SCOPE column, not a partition key — see the header note.']
  svsGodownId String [not null]
  svsItemId String [not null]
  svsLotId String [not null]
  svsBaseUomId String [not null, note: 'The item\'s base unit at freeze time. Carries NO foreign key in the
migration, unlike everywhere else in this module, so it stays scalar.']
  svsBucket String [not null, default: 'SALEABLE']
  svsSnapType String [not null, default: 'MONTH']
  svsSnapDate DateTime [not null]
  svsQty Decimal [not null, default: 0]
  svsAvgCostRate Decimal [not null, default: 0]
  svsAvgCostRateWot Decimal [not null, default: 0]
  svsValueAtCost Decimal [not null, default: 0]
  svsValueAtCostWot Decimal [not null, default: 0]
  svsValueAtMrp Decimal [not null, default: 0, note: 'What the shop floor counts. Unrecoverable once a price list changes.']
  svsValueAtSp Decimal [not null, default: 0, note: 'What a retailer means by "stock worth". Likewise unrecoverable.']
  svsBatchNo String
  svsMrp Decimal
  svsExpiryDate DateTime
  svsSupplierId String [note: 'No foreign key in the migration, unlike StockBalance.sblSupplierId — a
snapshot must survive the supplier row being restricted away.']
  svsAgeDays Int [note: 'How old the stock was ON the snapshot date. Stored because it cannot be
recomputed for a lot that has since been consumed.']
  svsFrozenOn DateTime [default: `now()`, not null]
  svsFrozenBy String
  svsIsDeleted Boolean [not null, default: false]
  svsSyncDate DateTime
  svsCreatedOn DateTime [default: `now()`, not null]
  company companys [not null]
  branch branch_master [not null]
  godown godown_locations [not null]
  item item_master [not null]
  lot stock_lot [not null]

  Note: 'stock.stock_valuation_snap — closing stock, FROZEN.

"Stock value as on 31 March" must give the same answer in April and in
October, and it must give it in seconds for fifty branches. Replaying
[[StockLedger]] does neither: it is slow, and a back-dated correction
posted in June would silently change March\'s audited figure. So the figure
is taken once at period close and never touched again.

AS-ON-DATE FOR ANY OTHER DAY is the nearest snapshot at or before it, plus
the ledger delta since — bounded work rather than a full replay. That is
what fn_stock_asof() does, and ix_svs_asof is the index it reads.

THREE VALUATIONS, NOT ONE, and this cannot be fixed later. At COST is what
the books want, at MRP is what the shop floor counts, at SELLING PRICE is
what a retailer means by "stock worth". Once a price list changes, the
second and third are unrecoverable for a date in the past — so all three
are frozen together or not at all.

svsAgeDays is stored for the same reason: it cannot be recomputed for a lot
that has since been consumed and closed.

svsAccYear IS A SCOPE COLUMN, NOT A PARTITION KEY. A snapshot is a frozen
fact that gets read for years, and a year-close snapshot is precisely what
the NEXT year opens against — partitioning it by year would put the two
sides of that handover in different partitions.

THE IDENTITY CACHE — svsBatchNo, svsMrp, svsExpiryDate, svsSupplierId — is
copied from [[StockLot]] at freeze time because the lot may be closed, and
its costs long superseded, by the time anybody reads this row.

WRITE RULES:
* A snapshot is WRITE-ONCE. Re-freezing a period means deleting the rows
for that (snapType, snapDate) and writing them again — never editing a
figure in place, which would make the audited number and the reported
number diverge with nothing to show for it.
* There is no modifiedOn/By pair, on purpose.

Vocabularies, enforced by CHECK constraints in the migration:
svsSnapType  DAY | MONTH | YEAR_OPEN | YEAR_CLOSE
svsBucket    SALEABLE | DAMAGED | QUARANTINE | EXPIRED | SAMPLE

DB-only objects Prisma cannot express — hand-authored in the migration and
deliberately NOT declared here (every one carries a WHERE):
ux_svs_scope   one row per holding per snapshot
ix_svs_asof    fn_stock_asof(): the nearest snapshot at or before a date
ix_svs_report  the valuation report: one branch, one date, everything'
}

Table stock_voucher {
  svhId String [not null]
  svhCompanyId String [not null]
  svhBranchId String [not null]
  svhTenantId String
  svhAccYear String [not null, note: 'The PARTITION KEY, and half the primary key.']
  svhDeviceId String [not null, note: 'The counter. See THE DEVICE IS THE COUNTER above.']
  svhSessionId String
  svhVoucherType String [not null]
  svhSlno BigInt [not null, note: 'Per company/branch/year/type/DEVICE — ux_svh_slno.']
  svhRefno String [not null, note: 'The printed number: unique per branch and year on its own — ux_svh_refno.']
  svhUsrRefno String
  svhDocDate DateTime [not null]
  svhDocDatetime DateTime [default: `now()`, not null]
  svhFromGodownId String
  svhToGodownId String
  svhToBranchId String
  svhSupplierId String
  svhPartyRef String
  svhReasonId String
  svhLinkSrcModule String
  svhLinkSrcDocType String
  svhLinkSrcDocId String
  svhLinkSrcAccYear String
  svhLineCount Int [not null, default: 0]
  svhTotalQty Decimal [not null, default: 0]
  svhTotalValue Decimal [not null, default: 0]
  svhTotalValueWot Decimal [not null, default: 0]
  svhStatus String [not null, default: 'DRAFT', note: 'The CURRENT state only. WHO moved it there, WHEN, and WHY is
public.txn_status_log\'s job — one row per step, and the only record of a
post or a cancellation there is. The svh_posted_on/_by, svh_approved_on/_by
and svh_cancelled_on/_by/svh_cancel_reason stamps that used to sit here
were a second, editable copy of the same facts; migration
20260909120000 backfilled the trail and dropped them. Read them back with
a lateral on txn_status_log (tsl_src_doc_id = svh_id, tsl_to_status), the
way getById and the list query do — never re-add a column here.']
  svhVersionNo Int [not null, default: 1]
  svhFreezeStock Boolean [not null, default: false, note: 'A count freezes the stock it is counting, or it counts a moving target.
ck_svh_freeze refuses a freeze with no window.']
  svhFreezeFrom DateTime
  svhFreezeTo DateTime
  svhRateSource String [note: 'Which rate the lines are valued at — see the vocabulary above.']
  svhRemarks String
  svhIsDeleted Boolean [not null, default: false]
  svhSyncDate DateTime
  svhCreatedOn DateTime [default: `now()`, not null]
  svhCreatedBy String
  svhModifiedOn DateTime
  svhModifiedBy String
  items stock_voucher_item [not null]
  company companys [not null]
  branch branch_master [not null]
  toBranch branch_master
  device device_master [not null, note: 'RESTRICT, not SET NULL: the device owns this document\'s number series, so
it can be blocked or deactivated but never unlinked.']
  fromGodown godown_locations
  toGodown godown_locations
  supplier suppliers
  reason stock_reason_master

  indexes {
    (svhId, svhAccYear) [pk]
  }

  Note: 'stock.stock_voucher — the DOCUMENT a stock movement is raised on. Eleven
voucher types, one table.

[[StockLedger]] records that stock moved; this records that somebody
DECIDED to move it — who, when, on what authority, with what remarks, and
with a printable number. A receipt, an issue, an adjustment, a transfer and
a physical count differ in which columns they fill, not in shape, so
splitting them into five tables would buy five sets of the same numbering,
posting, cancellation and print logic.

THE DEVICE IS THE COUNTER. There is no counter id: svhSlno is per
(company, branch, year, type, DEVICE), exactly as SaleOrder numbers itself,
because a voucher raised offline on a warehouse tablet must number itself
without asking a server it cannot reach. The printed svhRefno embeds the
device series and is unique per branch and year on its own.

NOTHING REACHES THE LEDGER UNTIL POSTED. A DRAFT is editable and moves no
stock; posting writes the ledger rows and is the point of no return —
afterwards a mistake is CANCELLED, which REVERSES, never deleted.
IN_TRANSIT is a TRANSFER_OUT that has left and not yet been received; see
[[StockTransit]] for why that state has to exist at all.

A TRANSFER IS TWO VOUCHERS, not one with two godowns — TRANSFER_OUT at the
source and TRANSFER_IN at the destination, each posting its own ledger row
in its own branch, and ck_svh_transfer_in_link refuses a TRANSFER_IN that
does not name the TRANSFER_OUT it answers. The link columns are the same
(module, docType, docId) triple used everywhere else and carry their own
year, because a transfer despatched on 29 March is received on 2 April.

PHYSICAL COUNTS FREEZE. svhFreezeStock with a from/to window is what stops
a count measuring a moving target; without it the difference posted is the
difference between a count taken at 6pm and a book figure read at 8pm.
ck_svh_freeze refuses a freeze with no window.

TOTALS ARE CACHES of [[StockVoucherItem]], which owns them. They exist so a
list screen does not aggregate the line table; when the two disagree, the
lines are right.

Vocabularies, enforced by CHECK constraints in the migration:
svhVoucherType  OPENING | RECEIPT | ISSUE | ADJUSTMENT | TRANSFER_OUT |
TRANSFER_IN | DAMAGE | EXPIRY_WRITEOFF | PHYSICAL |
REPACK_IN | REPACK_OUT
svhStatus       DRAFT | POSTED | IN_TRANSIT | RECEIVED | CANCELLED
svhRateSource   AVG_COST | LAST_PURCHASE | LOT_COST | MRP | MANUAL
plus ck_svh_acc_year (YYYY-YYYY, second = first + 1), ck_svh_godowns (a
movement must say where stock comes from or where it goes),
ck_svh_transfer_godowns (a transfer must say both), ck_svh_link (the source
triple is all-or-nothing) and ck_svh_slno.

PARTITIONED BY LIST (svh_acc_year), hence the composite primary key
(svhId, svhAccYear) — and hence [[StockVoucherItem]] joins back on BOTH
columns. A new fiscal year needs its partitions before the first save:
SELECT stock.fn_create_stock_partitions(\'2027-2028\');

DB-only objects Prisma cannot express — hand-authored in the migration and
deliberately NOT declared here. EVERY index on this table carries a WHERE,
and Prisma ignores indexes with predicates; declaring them would make
`migrate dev` regenerate them WITHOUT the predicate and fail on the
existing names:
ux_svh_slno    the per-DEVICE serial — offline numbering
ux_svh_refno   the per-branch printed number
ix_svh_list    the list screen, newest first, per type
ix_svh_inbound the transfer-in worklist: sent to me, not yet received
ix_svh_link    following a link back to the voucher that caused this one
Uniqueness on svhSlno / svhRefno is therefore app-side. Beware the DB error
if you rely on it: because the table is PARTITIONED, a duplicate is
reported against the PARTITION-LOCAL index name, never ux_svh_refno — match
on the column list in P2002 meta.target, or pre-check.'
}

Table stock_voucher_item {
  sviId String [not null]
  sviVoucherId String [not null]
  sviCompanyId String [not null]
  sviBranchId String [not null]
  sviTenantId String
  sviAccYear String [not null, note: 'The PARTITION KEY, half the primary key, AND half the foreign key back to
the voucher. Must equal the voucher\'s own year.']
  sviLineNo Int [not null]
  sviSplitNo Int [not null, default: 1, note: 'A second allocation of the same line — one line issued from three batches
is three rows. Only meaningful with a batch no (ck_svi_batch_split).']
  sviItemId String [not null]
  sviUomId String [not null, note: 'The unit keyed, and the item\'s base unit. BOTH are
item_unit_conversion(iuc_id), NEVER item_unit_master(unit_id).']
  sviBaseUomId String [not null]
  sviToBaseFactor Decimal [not null, default: 1]
  sviGodownId String [not null]
  sviLotId String [note: 'Resolved by fn_slt_resolve() at POSTING time. NULL only while DRAFT.']
  sviBucket String [not null, default: 'SALEABLE']
  sviBarcode String [note: 'What the scanner read, verbatim. NOT an identity: the item, batch and
serial were already resolved from it by the time the line is saved, and
nothing reads stock back through this column. It is kept so a reprint
can show the symbol that was actually scanned — a barcode retired from
the item master, or one that resolved through an alias, still prints as
what the storekeeper held under the gun.']
  sviBatchNo String
  sviMfgDate DateTime
  sviExpiryDate DateTime
  sviMrp Decimal
  sviSalePrice Decimal
  sviSerialNo String
  sviSupplierId String
  sviQty Decimal [not null, default: 0]
  sviBaseQty Decimal [not null, default: 0]
  sviFreeQty Decimal [not null, default: 0]
  sviFreeBaseQty Decimal [not null, default: 0]
  sviWeightQty Decimal [not null, default: 0]
  sviBookQty Decimal [note: 'What the system thought was there.']
  sviCountedQty Decimal [note: 'What was actually on the shelf.']
  sviDiffQty Decimal [note: 'GENERATED ALWAYS ... STORED as counted - book. What POSTS. Read-only,
never sent — the variance must not be editable.']
  sviCostRate Decimal [not null, default: 0]
  sviCostRateWot Decimal [not null, default: 0]
  sviLandedRate Decimal [not null, default: 0]
  sviTaxPerc Decimal [not null, default: 0]
  sviValue Decimal [note: 'GENERATED ALWAYS ... STORED as ROUND((baseQty + freeBaseQty) × rate, 2).
Read-only, never sent.']
  sviValueWot Decimal
  sviReasonId String
  sviRemarks String
  sviIsDeleted Boolean [not null, default: false]
  sviSyncDate DateTime
  sviCreatedOn DateTime [default: `now()`, not null]
  sviCreatedBy String
  sviModifiedOn DateTime
  sviModifiedBy String
  voucher stock_voucher [not null]
  item item_master [not null]
  uom item_unit_conversion [not null]
  baseUom item_unit_conversion [not null]
  godown godown_locations [not null]
  lot stock_lot
  supplier suppliers
  reason stock_reason_master

  indexes {
    (sviId, sviAccYear) [pk]
  }

  Note: 'stock.stock_voucher_item — the LINES of a [[StockVoucher]].

Same (lineNo, splitNo) grain as SaleBillItem, so one line issued from three
batches is three rows and [[StockLedger]] receives them unchanged: posting
is an INSERT ... SELECT with no reshaping and no aggregation. That symmetry
is the whole reason the grain is what it is.

THE BATCH ATTRIBUTES ARE STORED AS ENTERED as well as resolved to a lot.
sviBatchNo / sviMfgDate / sviExpiryDate / sviMrp / sviSalePrice /
sviSerialNo / sviSupplierId are what the storekeeper typed; sviLotId is
what fn_slt_resolve() made of it. Both are kept because a reprint must show
what was keyed, and because the resolver may LEGITIMATELY have blanked a
value the item\'s StockTrackPolicy does not track — an MRP typed on an item
that is not MRP-tracked does not become part of the lot identity, and
silently losing it would make the document unreadable a year later.

sviLotId IS NULL ONLY WHILE DRAFT. It is resolved at posting time, which is
also when the tracking policy is applied — so a draft saved before a policy
change posts under the policy live when it POSTS, not when it was keyed.

PHYSICAL COUNTS keep three numbers, not one: sviBookQty is what the system
thought, sviCountedQty is what was on the shelf, and sviDiffQty — GENERATED,
so it cannot be edited — is what actually posts to the ledger. Storing only
the difference makes a variance impossible to defend a year later; storing
it generated makes it impossible to fudge.

sviValue and sviValueWot are GENERATED from (baseQty + freeBaseQty) × rate,
rounded to 2, so a line\'s value cannot drift from its own quantity and rate.
Free goods are included: they are stock, and they cost something to hold.

WRITE RULES:
* NEVER write sviDiffQty, sviValue or sviValueWot — all three are
GENERATED ALWAYS ... STORED, and Postgres rejects any write to them.
* Quantities are MAGNITUDES; direction comes from the voucher type, not
from a sign here (ck_svi_qty_sign).
* ck_svi_batch_split mirrors ck_sbi_batch_split: a splitNo above 1 only
makes sense when the line is split BY BATCH, so it requires a batch no.
* sviAccYear must equal the voucher\'s — it is half the foreign key.

Vocabulary, enforced by a CHECK constraint in the migration:
sviBucket  SALEABLE | DAMAGED | QUARANTINE | EXPIRED | SAMPLE
plus ck_svi_acc_year, ck_svi_line_no, ck_svi_split_no,
ck_svi_to_base_factor and ck_svi_expiry_order.

PARTITIONED BY LIST (svi_acc_year), hence the composite primary key and the
COMPOSITE foreign key back to the voucher on (id, accYear) — a partitioned
parent can only be referenced through its partition key. ON DELETE CASCADE:
deleting a draft voucher takes its lines with it.

DB-only objects Prisma cannot express — hand-authored in the migration and
deliberately NOT declared here (every one carries a WHERE, and Prisma
ignores indexes with predicates):
ux_svi_line     one row per (voucher, year, lineNo, splitNo)
ix_svi_voucher  the lines of one voucher
ix_svi_item     item-wise across vouchers
ix_svi_lot      which documents touched a lot'
}

